WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:07.020
&gt;&gt; Good morning, everyone.

00:00:07.021 --> 00:00:08.740
Thank you so much for coming.

00:00:08.741 --> 00:00:10.440
My name is Tom Zimmermann,

00:00:10.441 --> 00:00:13.615
I'm a researcher in
the RiSE Group at MSR,

00:00:13.616 --> 00:00:16.210
and it's my pleasure to
introduce Andreas Zeller,

00:00:16.211 --> 00:00:19.975
who is a full professor at
Saarland University in Germany.

00:00:19.976 --> 00:00:21.680
And he was actually
my PhD adviser,

00:00:21.681 --> 00:00:24.500
so I'm really looking
forward to a talk.

00:00:24.501 --> 00:00:26.310
Andreas is one of

00:00:26.311 --> 00:00:29.145
the top people in
software engineering research.

00:00:29.146 --> 00:00:30.200
He is an ACM fellow.

00:00:30.201 --> 00:00:34.050
He won several Most Influential
Paper Awards for his work

00:00:34.051 --> 00:00:41.455
on mining software repositories
and automated debugging.

00:00:41.456 --> 00:00:43.540
In recent years, he has
done much more on security,

00:00:43.541 --> 00:00:46.540
and we are looking
forward to your talk.

00:00:46.541 --> 00:00:50.055
&gt;&gt;Thank you, Tom.
Thank you very much.

00:00:50.056 --> 00:00:51.990
I was making jokes
because I became

00:00:51.991 --> 00:00:55.710
ACM fellow for our work on
mining software repositories,

00:00:55.711 --> 00:00:57.075
among others, OK.

00:00:57.076 --> 00:00:59.550
Yes, that's great.

00:00:59.551 --> 00:01:02.865
It's always nice to come
back to the Seattle area.

00:01:02.866 --> 00:01:06.300
I just noted that it's now
18 years ago since I came here

00:01:06.301 --> 00:01:08.580
the first time and it's amazing

00:01:08.581 --> 00:01:11.520
how much things have
changed since then.

00:01:11.521 --> 00:01:15.540
I was strolling through
the city yesterday and I

00:01:15.541 --> 00:01:17.850
found that the Alaskan highway

00:01:17.851 --> 00:01:19.725
is now ripe for demolition,

00:01:19.726 --> 00:01:22.770
which people were talking
about 18 years ago.

00:01:22.771 --> 00:01:26.530
It's nice to see the new
520 bridge now in operation,

00:01:26.531 --> 00:01:31.020
which people were talking
about 18 years ago.

00:01:31.021 --> 00:01:32.250
&gt;&gt;The hardware is pretty
much the software.

00:01:32.251 --> 00:01:36.810
&gt;&gt;And I understand that
in only seven years,

00:01:36.811 --> 00:01:39.140
there will be
an extension to Microsoft.

00:01:39.141 --> 00:01:40.200
We will see, we'll see.

00:01:40.201 --> 00:01:42.150
Before I retire, I will see

00:01:42.151 --> 00:01:44.040
all the beautiful things

00:01:44.041 --> 00:01:46.470
that people have been talking
about all these years.

00:01:46.471 --> 00:01:47.990
&gt;&gt; You must plan the work.

00:01:47.991 --> 00:01:50.040
&gt;&gt;The place where I come

00:01:50.041 --> 00:01:52.445
from so this is
Germany over here,

00:01:52.446 --> 00:01:54.940
and this little pixel,

00:01:54.941 --> 00:01:57.505
two times two pixel thing here,

00:01:57.506 --> 00:01:58.845
this is the state of Saarland

00:01:58.846 --> 00:02:00.505
and this the City
of Saarbrucken.

00:02:00.506 --> 00:02:03.810
Well, I also arrived
there 16 years ago,

00:02:03.811 --> 00:02:07.710
and we've had no new bridges
or no new - oh,

00:02:07.711 --> 00:02:09.180
we got a new light rail.

00:02:09.181 --> 00:02:11.245
Yes, but it has
two new stations, too,

00:02:11.246 --> 00:02:13.655
very much like
the one over here.

00:02:13.656 --> 00:02:16.455
But, the place actually,

00:02:16.456 --> 00:02:19.200
but things don't
change that much.

00:02:19.201 --> 00:02:21.000
It's fine and it's good

00:02:21.001 --> 00:02:22.805
because things are
good as they are.

00:02:22.806 --> 00:02:24.660
Although there is
one particular point

00:02:24.661 --> 00:02:26.360
where things do change,

00:02:26.361 --> 00:02:29.265
and if you haven't been to
Saarbrucken for some time,

00:02:29.266 --> 00:02:31.825
this is our newest building
in computer science.

00:02:31.826 --> 00:02:35.280
This is our, oh I
think it should be,

00:02:35.281 --> 00:02:38.160
our tenth building or so
that we're getting there.

00:02:38.161 --> 00:02:40.815
This is an all new center for

00:02:40.816 --> 00:02:44.070
IT security and
earlier this year,

00:02:44.071 --> 00:02:45.780
it has been announced
that this center is going

00:02:45.781 --> 00:02:48.180
to be expanded massively.

00:02:48.181 --> 00:02:50.370
Right now, there's
about 100 researchers in there.

00:02:50.371 --> 00:02:51.960
It's going to expand to

00:02:51.961 --> 00:02:57.278
500 researchers in the course
of the next 10 years.

00:02:57.279 --> 00:02:59.400
Meaning that, right now,

00:02:59.401 --> 00:03:02.995
we're having one application
talk every week and it's

00:03:02.996 --> 00:03:04.260
going to remain that way for

00:03:04.261 --> 00:03:07.135
the next 10 years, which is fun.

00:03:07.136 --> 00:03:09.570
If you have any friend who,

00:03:09.571 --> 00:03:10.650
for some reason, doesn't like

00:03:10.651 --> 00:03:11.850
to go to Microsoft Research,

00:03:11.851 --> 00:03:14.295
which is also what I
recommend to my students,

00:03:14.296 --> 00:03:16.810
and who likes work in security,

00:03:16.811 --> 00:03:18.030
ask them to check us out.

00:03:18.031 --> 00:03:21.380
It's a very dynamic
place to be in.

00:03:21.381 --> 00:03:24.645
What I'm doing there is,

00:03:24.646 --> 00:03:27.415
among others, the work
that I'm presenting today.

00:03:27.416 --> 00:03:31.440
This is "Mining Input Grammars
for Security Testing".

00:03:31.441 --> 00:03:33.634
Although this is a bit, well,

00:03:33.635 --> 00:03:36.920
this is not the best title
for this talk,

00:03:36.921 --> 00:03:38.940
as I realized, unfortunately,

00:03:38.941 --> 00:03:41.520
only yesterday because
a much better title

00:03:41.521 --> 00:03:42.600
for this talk would be

00:03:42.601 --> 00:03:45.240
something like "Making $50,000

00:03:45.241 --> 00:03:47.420
a Month by Fuzzing Software".

00:03:47.421 --> 00:03:50.245
And now that I have
your attention,

00:03:50.246 --> 00:03:53.305
the money is a great motivator,

00:03:53.306 --> 00:03:57.463
let me tell you how
we're doing this.

00:03:57.464 --> 00:04:02.250
Fuzzing is what most of
you know I very much suppose,

00:04:02.251 --> 00:04:04.680
is the idea of feeding

00:04:04.681 --> 00:04:07.860
programs with random input
at the system level.

00:04:07.861 --> 00:04:10.290
This is Bart Miller who invented

00:04:10.291 --> 00:04:13.110
the term and
the technique in 1989,

00:04:13.111 --> 00:04:15.750
and the paper
today is a classic,

00:04:15.751 --> 00:04:19.560
"An Empirical Study of the
Reliability of UNIX Utilities",

00:04:19.561 --> 00:04:21.435
where Barton and

00:04:21.436 --> 00:04:25.110
his students went along
and created random inputs,

00:04:25.111 --> 00:04:28.140
fed them into the Unix
utilities at the time and

00:04:28.141 --> 00:04:31.650
saw them crashing and
hanging and actually,

00:04:31.651 --> 00:04:33.165
a very wide number,

00:04:33.166 --> 00:04:35.475
25 to 33 percent of them,

00:04:35.476 --> 00:04:37.230
because they were
never ever subject

00:04:37.231 --> 00:04:38.790
to such inputs before,

00:04:38.791 --> 00:04:40.710
actually crashed and hanged.

00:04:40.711 --> 00:04:42.840
This is a typical output
of a Fuzzer.

00:04:42.841 --> 00:04:44.265
It's very easy to
build a Fuzzer.

00:04:44.266 --> 00:04:47.430
Actually, that's
five lines of Python code,

00:04:47.431 --> 00:04:49.476
which will happily produce code,

00:04:49.477 --> 00:04:52.265
which will happily
produce inputs like these.

00:04:52.266 --> 00:04:53.610
I'm sure there is

00:04:53.611 --> 00:04:54.900
a programming language where

00:04:54.901 --> 00:04:56.520
this is actually
a valid program.

00:04:56.521 --> 00:04:58.190
&gt;&gt; It's called shots.

00:04:58.191 --> 00:05:02.280
&gt;&gt;Yeah, not exactly sure.

00:05:02.281 --> 00:05:03.395
Maybe we should, maybe we can

00:05:03.396 --> 00:05:05.070
fit this into
a Perl interpreter of

00:05:05.071 --> 00:05:08.194
sorts or something
else but the thing is,

00:05:08.195 --> 00:05:10.600
there will be lines in there,

00:05:10.601 --> 00:05:12.846
which simply are very long

00:05:12.847 --> 00:05:15.770
so programs are prone
to buffer overflows.

00:05:15.771 --> 00:05:17.895
Anything that parses
things will check, say,

00:05:17.896 --> 00:05:19.970
for strings, for
a beginning of a string,

00:05:19.971 --> 00:05:22.500
end of a string so there's
many ways that programs can

00:05:22.501 --> 00:05:26.120
fail simply by
scanning this input.

00:05:26.121 --> 00:05:27.960
And at the time, yes,

00:05:27.961 --> 00:05:31.045
plenty of different utilities

00:05:31.046 --> 00:05:33.615
on the various Unix
flavors of the time,

00:05:33.616 --> 00:05:35.940
on Sun, on Solaris, on IBM,

00:05:35.941 --> 00:05:38.656
and so on, all happily failed.

00:05:38.657 --> 00:05:40.830
Actually, the nice exception

00:05:40.831 --> 00:05:42.330
to the rule was the GNU tools,

00:05:42.331 --> 00:05:43.830
which already
existed at the time.

00:05:43.831 --> 00:05:45.925
They turned out to be
the most robust ones,

00:05:45.926 --> 00:05:47.880
and this is also

00:05:47.881 --> 00:05:51.195
the base for the tools
we have today on Linux.

00:05:51.196 --> 00:05:53.540
Since then, of course,

00:05:53.541 --> 00:05:55.260
1989 has been a long time,

00:05:55.261 --> 00:05:57.710
anyone now knows how
to fuzz a program.

00:05:57.711 --> 00:06:00.995
The reliability
has much improved.

00:06:00.996 --> 00:06:02.610
Today, it's actually
pretty hard to

00:06:02.611 --> 00:06:04.230
get any of these systems

00:06:04.231 --> 00:06:07.305
to crash if you use
the same technique

00:06:07.306 --> 00:06:10.720
as Bart Miller did in 1989

00:06:10.721 --> 00:06:16.440
but the thing is that
parsers that we have today,

00:06:16.441 --> 00:06:17.790
that are written
today, are much more

00:06:17.791 --> 00:06:20.220
robust than the parsers
we had in 1989,

00:06:20.221 --> 00:06:23.280
where people only began to
imagine that they could be

00:06:23.281 --> 00:06:28.437
some weird input that
feeds into one's program.

00:06:28.438 --> 00:06:31.770
The problem is that
a well-written parser will

00:06:31.771 --> 00:06:36.090
happily reject all
invalid input as invalid,

00:06:36.091 --> 00:06:38.355
and you will never ever reach

00:06:38.356 --> 00:06:40.800
the code that actually
matters to you,

00:06:40.801 --> 00:06:42.733
namely the runtime system,

00:06:42.734 --> 00:06:44.860
the deeper layers that
can be beyond the parse,

00:06:44.861 --> 00:06:46.535
where you would like to find

00:06:46.536 --> 00:06:51.715
bugs or whether you'd
like to find successes.

00:06:51.716 --> 00:06:55.980
However, if you do have
a syntactically valid input,

00:06:55.981 --> 00:06:58.080
that is, an input

00:06:58.081 --> 00:07:01.425
that is actually
processed by the parser,

00:07:01.426 --> 00:07:03.630
then you have a chance
to discover bugs in

00:07:03.631 --> 00:07:06.630
those later stages that happened

00:07:06.631 --> 00:07:12.465
that then process the input
as parsed by the parser.

00:07:12.466 --> 00:07:19.035
In order to get such a
syntactically valid input,

00:07:19.036 --> 00:07:21.330
what we need is

00:07:21.331 --> 00:07:23.826
a description of what
makes a valid input.

00:07:23.827 --> 00:07:26.955
This is called the input
language of the program,

00:07:26.956 --> 00:07:28.730
and we all know
there's language theory

00:07:28.731 --> 00:07:30.750
for that and what we can do is,

00:07:30.751 --> 00:07:33.060
we can create a grammar that

00:07:33.061 --> 00:07:36.620
describes the input structure,

00:07:36.621 --> 00:07:38.490
the syntactic rules
of the input,

00:07:38.491 --> 00:07:40.005
and using this grammar,

00:07:40.006 --> 00:07:44.490
we can then produce
a syntactically correct input.

00:07:44.491 --> 00:07:45.630
This is something we did,

00:07:45.631 --> 00:07:48.274
a student of mine in 2012.

00:07:48.275 --> 00:07:50.650
Now, that's five years ago.

00:07:50.651 --> 00:07:53.175
My student, Christian Holler,

00:07:53.176 --> 00:07:55.100
built this whole thing
for JavaScript.

00:07:55.101 --> 00:07:57.040
He built a JavaScript grammar,

00:07:57.041 --> 00:08:01.654
which actually was quite
some work writing this down.

00:08:01.655 --> 00:08:05.205
Using this JavaScript grammar,
which he created,

00:08:05.206 --> 00:08:08.295
his tool would
then happily create

00:08:08.296 --> 00:08:11.970
inputs that are indeed valid

00:08:11.971 --> 00:08:15.015
JavaScript and feed these to

00:08:15.016 --> 00:08:17.580
JavaScript parsers
such as the one that is

00:08:17.581 --> 00:08:21.145
found in Firefox or in Chrome.

00:08:21.146 --> 00:08:23.730
This input, as you
see it over here,

00:08:23.731 --> 00:08:26.070
was one of the first inputs
that actually crashed

00:08:26.071 --> 00:08:30.120
the parser or that crashed
the JavaScript interpreter,

00:08:30.121 --> 00:08:34.770
which actually is a serious
thing because if you can

00:08:34.771 --> 00:08:38.095
manage to crash an interpreter

00:08:38.096 --> 00:08:40.073
or the JavaScript interpreter,

00:08:40.074 --> 00:08:43.165
this typically is the base
for a later exploit.

00:08:43.166 --> 00:08:47.060
If you have an exploit in
the JavaScript interpreter,

00:08:47.061 --> 00:08:49.050
that's very serious
because this means that you

00:08:49.051 --> 00:08:50.880
can take over the entire browser

00:08:50.881 --> 00:08:56.235
and then collect
arbitrary data from the user.

00:08:56.236 --> 00:08:57.990
Browsers today are like

00:08:57.991 --> 00:09:01.400
operating systems so we get
full control of everything.

00:09:01.401 --> 00:09:04.140
&gt;&gt; The interesting thing
about the work

00:09:04.141 --> 00:09:06.540
of Christian, this fuzzer,

00:09:06.541 --> 00:09:09.315
was that it not only would it

00:09:09.316 --> 00:09:13.485
produce arbitrary random inputs,

00:09:13.486 --> 00:09:15.900
you could also use
the very same parser to

00:09:15.901 --> 00:09:19.190
parse existing
JavaScript programs,

00:09:19.191 --> 00:09:21.191
of which there were many.

00:09:21.192 --> 00:09:23.310
And to top it all off,

00:09:23.311 --> 00:09:25.635
he used his parser to parse

00:09:25.636 --> 00:09:28.980
existing JavaScript
programs from

00:09:28.981 --> 00:09:32.880
bug reports which were known
to have caused troubles

00:09:32.881 --> 00:09:35.250
before and he used them

00:09:35.251 --> 00:09:38.795
to parse them and then
to recombine them.

00:09:38.796 --> 00:09:41.580
So in a way he was looking
at reports of, say,

00:09:41.581 --> 00:09:45.936
the measles and cholera,
parsed these two,

00:09:45.937 --> 00:09:51.300
recombined them and created
new strains and feed all of

00:09:51.301 --> 00:09:54.180
these to the
interpreter in the hope

00:09:54.181 --> 00:09:58.530
of triggering new bugs
which was lots of fun.

00:09:58.531 --> 00:10:00.950
It also was very successful.

00:10:00.951 --> 00:10:02.940
Over the course of nine months,

00:10:02.941 --> 00:10:05.480
he got 18 Chromium
Security awards,

00:10:05.481 --> 00:10:09.270
12 Mozilla Bug Bounty
awards from

00:10:09.271 --> 00:10:12.380
Google and Mozilla respectively.

00:10:12.381 --> 00:10:16.875
And more specifically,
he gained $50,000

00:10:16.876 --> 00:10:19.350
in bug bounties in

00:10:19.351 --> 00:10:22.860
the first four weeks
of running his tool.

00:10:22.861 --> 00:10:24.485
Running his tool on
a single machine, on a laptop.

00:10:24.486 --> 00:10:25.500
So we're not even talking about

00:10:25.501 --> 00:10:27.425
a server farm or anything.

00:10:27.426 --> 00:10:29.480
And this is him,
this is Christian.

00:10:29.481 --> 00:10:32.090
After these four weeks,

00:10:32.091 --> 00:10:33.705
so you get to the top of

00:10:33.706 --> 00:10:37.390
all the security leaderboards

00:10:37.391 --> 00:10:39.954
and everything so that was fun,

00:10:39.955 --> 00:10:46.020
he got hired away by Mozilla
at a quite nice salary.

00:10:46.021 --> 00:10:49.890
It's not $50,000 a month
but it's steady, it's fun.

00:10:49.891 --> 00:10:51.591
And his job description
was simply to

00:10:51.592 --> 00:10:54.985
continue to run his tool.

00:10:54.986 --> 00:10:56.482
That was fun.

00:10:56.483 --> 00:11:01.530
So he's running his tool
for five years now.

00:11:01.531 --> 00:11:03.130
He's perfecting it.

00:11:03.131 --> 00:11:06.001
In these five years, no, sorry.

00:11:06.002 --> 00:11:11.760
Since- well, that's
one year ago.

00:11:11.761 --> 00:11:13.470
So one year ago when
I last asked him,

00:11:13.471 --> 00:11:17.360
his tool had found 4,000 bugs,

00:11:17.361 --> 00:11:20.280
had found 4,000 bugs in

00:11:20.281 --> 00:11:23.835
the JavaScript interpreters
of Firefox and Chrome,

00:11:23.836 --> 00:11:27.760
all of these bugs are
confirmed by developers, fixed.

00:11:27.761 --> 00:11:31.045
So all the shebang,
all is there.

00:11:31.046 --> 00:11:37.230
By the way, Mozilla gave him

00:11:37.231 --> 00:11:40.710
the authorization to
still run his tool on

00:11:40.711 --> 00:11:42.840
Chrome and he still gets

00:11:42.841 --> 00:11:45.854
bug bounties once a
while as a side business.

00:11:45.855 --> 00:11:47.190
So he gets a steady income

00:11:47.191 --> 00:11:48.600
from Mozilla and he gets
the bug bounties from Chrome

00:11:48.601 --> 00:11:54.564
the backbone so he's
perfectly at ease.

00:11:54.565 --> 00:12:00.630
&gt;&gt; Trigger buffer overruns?

00:12:00.631 --> 00:12:04.230
&gt;&gt; Yes, this is buffer overruns,
this is all sorts

00:12:04.231 --> 00:12:07.890
of ways to cause the interpreter
to hang or to crash.

00:12:07.891 --> 00:12:09.582
So it's buffer overruns,

00:12:09.583 --> 00:12:13.010
it's bugs in
the optimization stages,

00:12:13.011 --> 00:12:15.495
just-in-time
compiler, everything.

00:12:15.496 --> 00:12:16.807
And as I said,

00:12:16.808 --> 00:12:21.140
the largest success comes
from the fact that his tool

00:12:21.141 --> 00:12:23.460
is able to parse
existing bug reports and

00:12:23.461 --> 00:12:26.140
existing inputs
and recombine them.

00:12:26.141 --> 00:12:27.720
So not only are his inputs

00:12:27.721 --> 00:12:29.110
not completely
random but actually

00:12:29.111 --> 00:12:32.880
there's a stronger resemblance

00:12:32.881 --> 00:12:34.750
to existing code,
that's one thing.

00:12:34.751 --> 00:12:36.720
And the second thing
is that if you

00:12:36.721 --> 00:12:39.360
search in the vicinity
of existing bugs,

00:12:39.361 --> 00:12:42.855
you're likely to find
more bugs because as we know

00:12:42.856 --> 00:12:46.380
bug fixes aren't always perfect.

00:12:46.381 --> 00:12:48.150
Sometimes they treat
more the symptom

00:12:48.151 --> 00:12:50.208
than the cause and if you
search in the vicinity,

00:12:50.209 --> 00:12:51.360
you're going to find more.

00:12:51.361 --> 00:12:54.660
This is what made his tool
particularly successful.

00:12:54.661 --> 00:13:01.044
When a taxi driver asks me,

00:13:01.045 --> 00:13:02.910
so what do you do for a living?

00:13:02.911 --> 00:13:04.590
I'm a professor.

00:13:04.591 --> 00:13:09.409
So you're running from my
taxpayers money and everything,

00:13:09.410 --> 00:13:11.700
so have you already done

00:13:11.701 --> 00:13:15.890
something where you
can pay things back?

00:13:15.891 --> 00:13:17.800
Okay. You, at Microsoft,

00:13:17.801 --> 00:13:18.885
get asked this all the time

00:13:18.886 --> 00:13:21.120
but for me this is rather weird,

00:13:21.121 --> 00:13:22.909
then I can ask this taxi driver,

00:13:22.910 --> 00:13:24.400
so which browser are you using?

00:13:24.401 --> 00:13:26.760
Oh, I'm using Firefox and
I can go, yes, you know,

00:13:26.761 --> 00:13:28.410
it's because of my- the fact

00:13:28.411 --> 00:13:30.106
that you can use
Firefox and it's all

00:13:30.107 --> 00:13:33.948
safe is well to some extent,

00:13:33.949 --> 00:13:36.000
to quite some extent
actually due

00:13:36.001 --> 00:13:38.530
to our work with
my student at the time.

00:13:38.531 --> 00:13:42.410
And then he's very happy
and everything is settled.

00:13:42.411 --> 00:13:46.305
So this is work that's
already a couple of years ago.

00:13:46.306 --> 00:13:48.380
It was very nice, we loved it

00:13:48.381 --> 00:13:50.935
and also had
a nice paper out of it.

00:13:50.936 --> 00:13:55.200
So in principle one

00:13:55.201 --> 00:13:57.204
could do this for every program

00:13:57.205 --> 00:13:59.280
but the problem is that, well,

00:13:59.281 --> 00:14:02.250
you need a grammar in
the first place and creating

00:14:02.251 --> 00:14:07.495
such a grammar for a
for a non-trivial input,

00:14:07.496 --> 00:14:09.970
most inputs are non-trivial,

00:14:09.971 --> 00:14:11.620
it's actually quite some work.

00:14:11.621 --> 00:14:14.160
For Christian, it took
him three months to

00:14:14.161 --> 00:14:17.004
create this JavaScript grammar.

00:14:17.005 --> 00:14:22.710
Actually, if you want to have
a real working grammar for

00:14:22.711 --> 00:14:25.890
C programs for instance
just in order to do

00:14:25.891 --> 00:14:27.660
some refactoring work or like

00:14:27.661 --> 00:14:30.475
things such that you have
a good parser for it,

00:14:30.476 --> 00:14:32.370
that's actually something
quite expensive.

00:14:32.371 --> 00:14:33.450
I think you have
to shell out about

00:14:33.451 --> 00:14:35.415
$10,000 for a single license

00:14:35.416 --> 00:14:38.940
for a good C grammar
for instance.

00:14:38.941 --> 00:14:42.835
Yes?

00:14:42.836 --> 00:14:42.957
&gt;&gt; But in some sense,

00:14:42.958 --> 00:14:43.719
you don't need
a really good grammar,

00:14:43.720 --> 00:14:44.115
you just need
a good enough grammar.

00:14:44.116 --> 00:14:45.210
So you could write

00:14:45.211 --> 00:14:47.130
a grammar that is much more
permissive than it needs

00:14:47.131 --> 00:14:50.630
to be and rely on a real parser
to allow that [inaudible].

00:14:50.631 --> 00:14:52.975
&gt;&gt; To some extent, yes.

00:14:52.976 --> 00:14:55.050
If you want to
have a grammar that

00:14:55.051 --> 00:14:57.350
simply produces lots
of JavaScript code,

00:14:57.351 --> 00:14:59.460
you can deal with a grammar

00:14:59.461 --> 00:15:03.710
that only deals with a subset
of JavaScript for instance.

00:15:03.711 --> 00:15:05.460
But if you want
the very same grammar to

00:15:05.461 --> 00:15:07.880
parse the existing inputs
and to recombine them,

00:15:07.881 --> 00:15:09.360
it really needs to go down to

00:15:09.361 --> 00:15:11.370
the tiniest details
in order to be

00:15:11.371 --> 00:15:16.440
able to process all the-

00:15:16.441 --> 00:15:16.556
&gt;&gt; You could always use
that real parser to filter out-

00:15:16.557 --> 00:15:17.600
&gt;&gt; That's right. That's right.

00:15:17.601 --> 00:15:23.180
So there's two things in here.

00:15:23.181 --> 00:15:25.440
So your grammar may

00:15:25.441 --> 00:15:27.780
over approximate that
is it may produce

00:15:27.781 --> 00:15:30.510
more inputs that are
actually not valid

00:15:30.511 --> 00:15:34.320
JavaScript then in this
particular setting of fuzzing.

00:15:34.321 --> 00:15:36.750
Well you're going to
live with whatever 20%,

00:15:36.751 --> 00:15:38.700
50% of inputs that will

00:15:38.701 --> 00:15:41.190
simply be rejected so
that's all there is.

00:15:41.191 --> 00:15:42.970
You're just wasting
a bit of time.

00:15:42.971 --> 00:15:46.254
But if you have a grammar
that under approximates,

00:15:46.255 --> 00:15:48.390
you're going to miss out
a couple of features which will

00:15:48.391 --> 00:15:49.470
not be tested and then you will

00:15:49.471 --> 00:15:50.610
miss all the bugs
that are in there.

00:15:50.611 --> 00:15:53.250
So that's the downside
of the whole thing.

00:15:53.251 --> 00:15:54.690
So there's these two sides.

00:15:54.691 --> 00:15:57.600
But then we're
talking about context

00:15:57.601 --> 00:15:59.055
free grammars here anyway,

00:15:59.056 --> 00:16:01.020
and Javascript has plenty of

00:16:01.021 --> 00:16:03.425
context sensitive features
as any programming language.

00:16:03.426 --> 00:16:05.160
Actually, Christian
also did a couple

00:16:05.161 --> 00:16:07.080
of tricks in order to
deal with identifiers

00:16:07.081 --> 00:16:14.695
and others in order to have
the parser accept more input.

00:16:14.696 --> 00:16:22.400
So, what we started to
reason about at the time was

00:16:22.401 --> 00:16:27.705
the idea of whether
it would be possible

00:16:27.706 --> 00:16:36.325
to learn such grammars given
not only a set of samples.

00:16:36.326 --> 00:16:38.325
There's a couple of works to

00:16:38.326 --> 00:16:40.520
do that using machine
learning but actually

00:16:40.521 --> 00:16:42.410
using the program at hand that

00:16:42.411 --> 00:16:44.780
processes those inputs
in order to

00:16:44.781 --> 00:16:49.280
learn how it's inputs
are structured.

00:16:49.281 --> 00:16:53.305
And the way we built
it is as follows.

00:16:53.306 --> 00:16:56.970
We do have a set of inputs
which can be a very small set.

00:16:56.971 --> 00:16:58.160
Actually, we're aiming to get

00:16:58.161 --> 00:17:00.985
this set to be the empty set.

00:17:00.986 --> 00:17:04.138
We feed these inputs
to a program.

00:17:04.139 --> 00:17:09.103
We observe how the program
processes these inputs,

00:17:09.104 --> 00:17:11.890
we do this dynamically.

00:17:11.891 --> 00:17:13.905
We have a tool called autogram,

00:17:13.906 --> 00:17:17.040
which observes how
the program processes

00:17:17.041 --> 00:17:18.870
those inputs and out of

00:17:18.871 --> 00:17:21.935
these observations it
produces a grammar.

00:17:21.936 --> 00:17:26.370
This grammar can then
be fed into a parser.

00:17:26.371 --> 00:17:28.920
which then happily will
create more inputs towards

00:17:28.921 --> 00:17:31.590
the program thus
creating in the end

00:17:31.591 --> 00:17:33.870
a virtual circle where
you learn more and more

00:17:33.871 --> 00:17:37.440
about the grammar and
the program itself.

00:17:37.441 --> 00:17:40.275
So how does this work?

00:17:40.276 --> 00:17:43.140
Think of a program

00:17:43.141 --> 00:17:45.990
that processes a very simple
input, let's say, URLs.

00:17:45.991 --> 00:17:48.582
We all know what
URLs are made of

00:17:48.583 --> 00:17:51.326
and the idea is as follows.

00:17:51.327 --> 00:17:53.670
A URL is made out
of different parts.

00:17:53.671 --> 00:17:55.315
We do have a protocol.

00:17:55.316 --> 00:17:56.735
We do have the hostname.

00:17:56.736 --> 00:17:58.415
We do have a port.

00:17:58.416 --> 00:18:00.395
We do have a login.

00:18:00.396 --> 00:18:02.724
People, you should
not use logins in URLs

00:18:02.725 --> 00:18:06.132
because they're transmitted
in the clear but,

00:18:06.133 --> 00:18:08.426
well, they provide this,

00:18:08.427 --> 00:18:10.375
and there's also path.

00:18:10.376 --> 00:18:12.480
So these are standard
elements and we do have

00:18:12.481 --> 00:18:15.500
a couple of separators in there.

00:18:15.501 --> 00:18:18.480
And all of these elements
while they are being

00:18:18.481 --> 00:18:23.150
processed by the program
that processes a URL,

00:18:23.151 --> 00:18:25.890
all of these
individual fragments

00:18:25.891 --> 00:18:29.670
go through different paths
and execution.

00:18:29.671 --> 00:18:31.605
So the protocol, for instance,

00:18:31.606 --> 00:18:33.490
the protocol is being used to to

00:18:33.491 --> 00:18:35.925
determine what is
going to be parsed,

00:18:35.926 --> 00:18:37.833
how the rest is
going to be parsed.

00:18:37.834 --> 00:18:40.290
The hostname is
going to end up in

00:18:40.291 --> 00:18:44.310
some DNS resolver function
that is going to

00:18:44.311 --> 00:18:46.500
return us an IP address to which

00:18:46.501 --> 00:18:49.100
we are going to connect
using the given port.

00:18:49.101 --> 00:18:50.550
And then over that connection,

00:18:50.551 --> 00:18:52.185
we're going to send out

00:18:52.186 --> 00:18:55.485
the login as well
as the page request.

00:18:55.486 --> 00:18:57.600
And all of these data,

00:18:57.601 --> 00:19:00.660
all of these little fragments
go through different paths

00:19:00.661 --> 00:19:02.190
through the execution
and they end

00:19:02.191 --> 00:19:04.131
up in different functions.

00:19:04.132 --> 00:19:10.050
And this idea that there
is a natural separation of

00:19:10.051 --> 00:19:13.485
concerns in the program
execution that corresponds

00:19:13.486 --> 00:19:17.830
to the structure in the input
is what we want to exploit.

00:19:17.831 --> 00:19:20.325
So all of these
different functions,

00:19:20.326 --> 00:19:22.200
for each fragment
different functions that are

00:19:22.201 --> 00:19:24.347
stored in different variables,

00:19:24.348 --> 00:19:28.110
this is the basic idea
where we want to

00:19:28.111 --> 00:19:32.555
be able to extract
the structure of the grammar.

00:19:32.556 --> 00:19:35.485
So, how can we do that?

00:19:35.486 --> 00:19:37.825
Essentially we have
explored two ways.

00:19:37.826 --> 00:19:42.485
One very thorough one
and one very simple one.

00:19:42.486 --> 00:19:47.120
The first technique that we
looked at is to use dynamic

00:19:47.121 --> 00:19:50.450
tainting which
essentially dynamically

00:19:50.451 --> 00:19:52.830
during execution
attaches a label,

00:19:52.831 --> 00:19:57.350
a tag, to individual data

00:19:57.351 --> 00:19:59.441
as elements as they're
being processed.

00:19:59.442 --> 00:20:02.440
And this tag propagates
along the computation.

00:20:02.441 --> 00:20:06.260
So for instance, and this tag in

00:20:06.261 --> 00:20:10.365
our case labels all the inputs
with their origin,

00:20:10.366 --> 00:20:13.040
that is the input source and
the position in the input.

00:20:13.041 --> 00:20:15.020
So for instance, we can
track where the port goes

00:20:15.021 --> 00:20:17.956
to and even if the port
gets converted to a number,

00:20:17.957 --> 00:20:19.400
the number inherits the tag of

00:20:19.401 --> 00:20:21.770
the string and the port
then carries that,

00:20:21.771 --> 00:20:24.800
take over to the function so
we can track all of these.

00:20:24.801 --> 00:20:27.099
We can track all of
these inputs down.

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:30.265
The second technique
is much simpler.

00:20:30.266 --> 00:20:32.780
This simply consists of

00:20:32.781 --> 00:20:35.810
taking a memory dump at
a particular point in time.

00:20:35.811 --> 00:20:38.750
And simply looking for
all string variables,

00:20:38.751 --> 00:20:40.580
whether any of
the string variables

00:20:40.581 --> 00:20:41.840
contain some characters

00:20:41.841 --> 00:20:46.545
that also happened to occur
in the original input.

00:20:46.546 --> 00:20:49.490
This is much simpler,
it's much cruder.

00:20:49.491 --> 00:20:51.695
There's also room
for error in there,

00:20:51.696 --> 00:20:55.070
but it turns out that
the latter one is something that

00:20:55.071 --> 00:20:57.710
one can actually implement

00:20:57.711 --> 00:21:00.130
much easier than the first one.

00:21:00.131 --> 00:21:02.750
And I had some fun
a couple of weeks

00:21:02.751 --> 00:21:06.275
ago implementing
the second technique in Python.

00:21:06.276 --> 00:21:11.165
In Python, tracking a variable

00:21:11.166 --> 00:21:12.520
is very- in tracking- sorry.

00:21:12.521 --> 00:21:14.285
In Python,

00:21:14.286 --> 00:21:16.460
accessing memory
during execution

00:21:16.461 --> 00:21:18.840
is a very simple exercise.

00:21:18.841 --> 00:21:23.870
You do have a function that
is called settrace and you

00:21:23.871 --> 00:21:26.810
pass this- and you have
this API call settrace

00:21:26.811 --> 00:21:29.335
and you pass it to function
which is called traceit.

00:21:29.336 --> 00:21:32.060
And this traceit function
is going to be

00:21:32.061 --> 00:21:34.430
called for every
single line that is

00:21:34.431 --> 00:21:37.830
executed and every line

00:21:37.831 --> 00:21:42.530
you get past the current frame,
the current event.

00:21:42.531 --> 00:21:43.640
Meaning that a new line has been

00:21:43.641 --> 00:21:45.740
execute as well as
other information.

00:21:45.741 --> 00:21:47.640
And in the current frame
you can then look

00:21:47.641 --> 00:21:50.300
up all the local variables and

00:21:50.301 --> 00:21:55.455
all of their values as Python
elements. It's very simple.

00:21:55.456 --> 00:21:57.860
So actually writing
a debugger in

00:21:57.861 --> 00:22:01.030
Python is 12 lines of code.

00:22:01.031 --> 00:22:05.613
Okay? So, compare
that to Java or C,

00:22:05.614 --> 00:22:08.625
it's no fun at all.

00:22:08.626 --> 00:22:12.630
In Python, dynamic
analysis is a breeze.

00:22:12.631 --> 00:22:16.535
And what we do is we access
all the local variables,

00:22:16.536 --> 00:22:18.380
we access all of the values

00:22:18.381 --> 00:22:20.210
and then we simply
check for the values.

00:22:20.211 --> 00:22:25.070
If it's a string and if it
has two characters or more,

00:22:25.071 --> 00:22:26.600
and if this value also

00:22:26.601 --> 00:22:28.580
occurs in the input
that we've seen,

00:22:28.581 --> 00:22:32.420
then we simply store
the variable that we have.

00:22:32.421 --> 00:22:34.730
Store the variable and the value
that we have just seen.

00:22:34.731 --> 00:22:36.577
So we just track these.

00:22:36.578 --> 00:22:41.010
And let me show you how
this works in a real code.

00:22:41.011 --> 00:22:44.591
So here's the very
same code again.

00:22:44.592 --> 00:22:46.690
I do have a function
which is your URL parse.

00:22:46.691 --> 00:22:49.310
I do have a couple of inputs
which are passed to them.

00:22:49.311 --> 00:22:52.400
And very down here,

00:22:52.401 --> 00:22:56.380
I should have
precisely the function

00:22:56.381 --> 00:22:57.430
that we have just seen.

00:22:57.431 --> 00:22:59.830
So I'm using this
trace function thing.

00:22:59.831 --> 00:23:00.800
Where is it again?

00:23:00.801 --> 00:23:03.720
So one second. Here we go.

00:23:03.721 --> 00:23:07.060
So this is the set
tracing function.

00:23:07.061 --> 00:23:09.910
I am producing some output

00:23:09.911 --> 00:23:12.880
and for every value
I'm saving it

00:23:12.881 --> 00:23:15.460
and accessing the
individual variables and I'm

00:23:15.461 --> 00:23:19.750
saving those that also
occur in the input.

00:23:19.751 --> 00:23:22.082
Now, to run this
on your URL parse,

00:23:22.083 --> 00:23:24.210
this is precisely what I get.

00:23:24.211 --> 00:23:26.632
So I get that during
your URL path,

00:23:26.633 --> 00:23:28.555
a function which I
have not implemented,

00:23:28.556 --> 00:23:30.460
which I have never seen.

00:23:30.461 --> 00:23:31.120
Actually, this would be

00:23:31.121 --> 00:23:32.875
something I've never
looked into that,

00:23:32.876 --> 00:23:36.130
there's a variable called
URL which holds the path.

00:23:36.131 --> 00:23:38.460
This is one part of the input.

00:23:38.461 --> 00:23:41.180
There is a variable
named fragment

00:23:41.181 --> 00:23:44.165
which lo and behold
it's the fragment.

00:23:44.166 --> 00:23:46.150
There is a variable called

00:23:46.151 --> 00:23:48.910
scheme which happens
to be the scheme.

00:23:48.911 --> 00:23:50.920
There is a variable
called netloc which holds

00:23:50.921 --> 00:23:53.740
the host and the port.

00:23:53.741 --> 00:23:55.925
Which is precisely what
we have over here.

00:23:55.926 --> 00:23:58.690
And there there's a path which
is very much like the URL

00:23:58.691 --> 00:24:03.250
also holds this
very path over here.

00:24:03.251 --> 00:24:05.080
And I can now go and
also apply this to

00:24:05.081 --> 00:24:07.690
other inputs just
for the fun of it.

00:24:07.691 --> 00:24:11.865
Where I will, if you let me,
make this the first input.

00:24:11.866 --> 00:24:14.115
So here I am happy
lurking along.

00:24:14.116 --> 00:24:17.380
So here's another URL and
here we also have a scheme

00:24:17.381 --> 00:24:21.100
which now is HTTPs and
we do have a netloc,

00:24:21.101 --> 00:24:24.036
again, a location which
also occurs in here.

00:24:24.037 --> 00:24:26.740
So you can see that simply
by looking at the variables,

00:24:26.741 --> 00:24:28.060
I can already extract

00:24:28.061 --> 00:24:30.520
the individual
lexical items as they

00:24:30.521 --> 00:24:36.040
occur in the input.

00:24:36.041 --> 00:24:41.655
So from this I can
now go and infer

00:24:41.656 --> 00:24:45.140
and use this knowledge
that the input is

00:24:45.141 --> 00:24:48.934
decomposed into these variables
to derive a grammar.

00:24:48.935 --> 00:24:50.640
Namely in two steps,

00:24:50.641 --> 00:24:53.115
I start with a very simple
grammar that simply says,

00:24:53.116 --> 00:24:55.740
"My grammar consists of
precisely this input."

00:24:55.741 --> 00:24:57.490
This is very trivial,

00:24:57.491 --> 00:25:00.040
anybody can write
example like this.

00:25:00.041 --> 00:25:02.625
Then you create a disjunction
over multiple inserts and

00:25:02.626 --> 00:25:05.300
it's all very simple.
That's not how you do it.

00:25:05.301 --> 00:25:08.150
Okay, well. And what we do then,

00:25:08.151 --> 00:25:11.420
no grammar inference can
be a very easy thing.

00:25:11.421 --> 00:25:15.135
It's actually
100% sound, so it's cool.

00:25:15.136 --> 00:25:19.048
Every string in that grammar
is a valid input.

00:25:19.049 --> 00:25:21.675
So and then what I do is I go,

00:25:21.676 --> 00:25:24.030
and for each and every variable
I see,I introduce

00:25:24.031 --> 00:25:28.050
the rule by replacing
all the occurrences

00:25:28.051 --> 00:25:31.830
of that value by

00:25:31.831 --> 00:25:33.990
a non-terminal and
then I say this

00:25:33.991 --> 00:25:36.423
non-terminal expands to
the appropriate value.

00:25:36.424 --> 00:25:39.090
Let's try that out with
the values that we've just seen.

00:25:39.091 --> 00:25:42.020
So here's my initial grammar.

00:25:42.021 --> 00:25:45.270
Here we go. Now I
apply the second rule.

00:25:45.271 --> 00:25:46.740
I go and all of

00:25:46.741 --> 00:25:50.150
these variables I introduce
appropriate new rules.

00:25:50.151 --> 00:25:52.415
So let's start with
netloc for instance.

00:25:52.416 --> 00:25:54.010
netloc, this is this one.

00:25:54.011 --> 00:25:56.610
So I'm going to replace
this string as it occurs up

00:25:56.611 --> 00:25:59.428
here by the netloc.

00:25:59.429 --> 00:26:00.890
So this is what I get.

00:26:00.891 --> 00:26:03.185
So now I have
this expansion to netloc

00:26:03.186 --> 00:26:05.820
and I see what
netloc is in here.

00:26:05.821 --> 00:26:08.305
I can do the same
for the scheme.

00:26:08.306 --> 00:26:10.500
I can repeat the same
thing, for the path,

00:26:10.501 --> 00:26:13.455
for the fragment
as well as finally

00:26:13.456 --> 00:26:16.490
for this URL variable which
doesn't really belong here.

00:26:16.491 --> 00:26:18.165
I guess, it's
some sort of misnomer,

00:26:18.166 --> 00:26:19.755
but that's what you get.

00:26:19.756 --> 00:26:21.450
And if I do this again then I

00:26:21.451 --> 00:26:23.206
see that the path
is in fact a URL.

00:26:23.207 --> 00:26:24.860
Under URL there's path.

00:26:24.861 --> 00:26:30.055
All these names are well-chosen
except for the URL one.

00:26:30.056 --> 00:26:32.670
I have no idea what the folks in

00:26:32.671 --> 00:26:34.020
URL parse thought of when

00:26:34.021 --> 00:26:35.890
they gave this
variable this name.

00:26:35.891 --> 00:26:38.430
But what you can see
here now is how we have

00:26:38.431 --> 00:26:41.895
decompose this grammar input
into a nice of things.

00:26:41.896 --> 00:26:46.230
So our URL starts with
a scheme followed by location,

00:26:46.231 --> 00:26:49.238
followed by a path,
followed by a fragment.

00:26:49.239 --> 00:26:55.490
Now, this is just for
one single input, okay?

00:26:55.491 --> 00:26:57.070
And of course I
want to extend this

00:26:57.071 --> 00:27:01.255
to multiple inputs. Yes.

00:27:01.256 --> 00:27:04.160
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] for
example if the user had

00:27:04.161 --> 00:27:07.565
capitalized all the URLs?

00:27:07.566 --> 00:27:09.425
&gt;&gt; Yes, yes, yes. I'll
come to that in a second.

00:27:09.426 --> 00:27:11.290
Wait a moment.

00:27:11.291 --> 00:27:14.370
We'll definitely expand
this whole thing, okay?

00:27:14.371 --> 00:27:16.645
So this is just for
one single input

00:27:16.646 --> 00:27:18.940
and this is just
one decomposition,

00:27:18.941 --> 00:27:21.810
the one decomposition
we've seen for that input.

00:27:21.811 --> 00:27:24.970
Now the idea is
that I can go and

00:27:24.971 --> 00:27:28.675
do this for multiple
inputs, sorry.

00:27:28.676 --> 00:27:31.115
And for multiple inputs,

00:27:31.116 --> 00:27:33.860
what I'm going to- Actually

00:27:33.861 --> 00:27:35.130
I have implemented
this whole thing.

00:27:35.131 --> 00:27:36.950
So here we go.

00:27:36.951 --> 00:27:39.700
And if we implement
this and if we run this,

00:27:39.701 --> 00:27:42.010
this's precisely
the grammar that we get.

00:27:42.011 --> 00:27:44.230
So this is just
live here in Python.

00:27:44.231 --> 00:27:47.185
Note that this is a fully
self-contained Python program

00:27:47.186 --> 00:27:48.880
and we are at like 200 here.

00:27:48.881 --> 00:27:55.650
So it took me
four hours to design it,

00:27:55.651 --> 00:27:57.660
to build it, and to
quality control it.

00:27:57.661 --> 00:28:01.445
So that was something that
was not very hard to do

00:28:01.446 --> 00:28:03.510
and this was very annoying
for my student who had

00:28:03.511 --> 00:28:06.680
spent a year building
this whole thing for Java,

00:28:06.681 --> 00:28:08.490
but his work is much more

00:28:08.491 --> 00:28:09.690
sophisticated than the very

00:28:09.691 --> 00:28:12.060
crude thing I'm
having over here.

00:28:12.061 --> 00:28:13.410
Now the interesting thing is

00:28:13.411 --> 00:28:16.350
that I can repeat this thing for

00:28:16.351 --> 00:28:18.540
multiple inputs and I

00:28:18.541 --> 00:28:21.090
can easily merge
these grammars together.

00:28:21.091 --> 00:28:24.980
And since I have the structure
I can merge them by rule.

00:28:24.981 --> 00:28:26.940
So for every single rule
that I have,

00:28:26.941 --> 00:28:28.410
I'm simply going to
add a disjunction

00:28:28.411 --> 00:28:30.015
for the next value that I see

00:28:30.016 --> 00:28:33.655
which actually represents
the individual variables,

00:28:33.656 --> 00:28:35.310
the individual values that

00:28:35.311 --> 00:28:37.573
their respective
variable can take.

00:28:37.574 --> 00:28:39.660
So if I do that I am going

00:28:39.661 --> 00:28:41.870
to spare you the code for that.

00:28:41.871 --> 00:28:44.350
This is not very nice code,

00:28:44.351 --> 00:28:46.551
but I'm simply
going to run that.

00:28:46.552 --> 00:28:49.890
So what I have done now
here is I have taken

00:28:49.891 --> 00:28:53.730
three inputs with
different schemes for instance.

00:28:53.731 --> 00:28:55.780
And how you see, here
is the disjunction,

00:28:55.781 --> 00:28:58.380
it's HTTP or HTTPS and

00:28:58.381 --> 00:29:01.965
the fragment variables one
is ref and one is ref2.

00:29:01.966 --> 00:29:04.200
And the host of course can take

00:29:04.201 --> 00:29:06.915
different values and the query,

00:29:06.916 --> 00:29:07.980
we have now other query.

00:29:07.981 --> 00:29:09.635
This is one of the inputs.

00:29:09.636 --> 00:29:11.725
Well, there's only
just one variable here.

00:29:11.726 --> 00:29:13.740
So we have a path that

00:29:13.741 --> 00:29:17.620
has taken the value
/path and /bar.

00:29:17.621 --> 00:29:21.370
And over here we see the
various schemes that there are.

00:29:21.371 --> 00:29:25.860
So we see that you
can have something.

00:29:25.861 --> 00:29:27.420
With a fragment you
can have something.

00:29:27.421 --> 00:29:29.010
Without a fragment,
you can have something.

00:29:29.011 --> 00:29:30.845
With a query and a fragment.

00:29:30.846 --> 00:29:32.585
So these are different
expansions and if

00:29:32.586 --> 00:29:34.830
I would add
some syntactic sugar,

00:29:34.831 --> 00:29:36.150
I could go and say

00:29:36.151 --> 00:29:38.220
that the query in
here and the fragment

00:29:38.221 --> 00:29:42.870
are actually both optional.

00:29:42.871 --> 00:29:44.785
Now if I have such a grammar

00:29:44.786 --> 00:29:47.470
and indeed I can start
with this very grammar,

00:29:47.471 --> 00:29:53.065
I can immediately use
this for producing

00:29:53.066 --> 00:29:55.360
various combinations which then

00:29:55.361 --> 00:29:58.735
gives us various combinations
of the little constituents.

00:29:58.736 --> 00:30:03.370
And then I can create
arbitrary combinations of hosts,

00:30:03.371 --> 00:30:04.885
and paths,

00:30:04.886 --> 00:30:08.888
and references and for
some reason no query in here.

00:30:08.889 --> 00:30:10.660
Good. I'm going to-
where's with the query?

00:30:10.661 --> 00:30:12.010
Oh yes, hasn't expanded.

00:30:12.011 --> 00:30:15.070
Let's try that again.
Do we see a query?

00:30:15.071 --> 00:30:18.190
No query. How come?
Good. Here we go.

00:30:18.191 --> 00:30:20.106
Again, another combination.

00:30:20.107 --> 00:30:23.110
So every time I can
use this roll the dice,

00:30:23.111 --> 00:30:25.300
use an arbitrary
extension writing

00:30:25.301 --> 00:30:28.055
a producer for a given grammar.

00:30:28.056 --> 00:30:29.785
Again, this is
eight lines of code.

00:30:29.786 --> 00:30:31.465
Not very complicated.

00:30:31.466 --> 00:30:34.480
Okay. And of course the more
elaborated my grammar gets,

00:30:34.481 --> 00:30:35.770
the more alternatives I have and

00:30:35.771 --> 00:30:40.845
the more possibilities
for combination follow.

00:30:40.846 --> 00:30:43.065
So as I said, this is
a very simple program.

00:30:43.066 --> 00:30:44.980
We use these programs
for teaching.

00:30:44.981 --> 00:30:47.550
I'm having a course
on security testing,

00:30:47.551 --> 00:30:51.055
the next one starts
mid-October and we are

00:30:51.056 --> 00:30:52.930
indeed showing folks how to

00:30:52.931 --> 00:30:55.150
create such grammars and
how to mine such grammars.

00:30:55.151 --> 00:30:56.620
This is the very program
we're giving out to

00:30:56.621 --> 00:30:58.645
students such that they can then

00:30:58.646 --> 00:31:04.520
try out and see
how it works. Now-

00:31:04.521 --> 00:31:11.570
&gt;&gt;This is just a very
simple version, okay?

00:31:11.571 --> 00:31:13.370
There is still lots
and lots of pieces

00:31:13.371 --> 00:31:16.640
missing but it is clear that

00:31:16.641 --> 00:31:19.987
there's ways to
further expand this and

00:31:19.988 --> 00:31:23.768
we now get to
our real implementation,

00:31:23.769 --> 00:31:25.700
the one that my student
took a year for.

00:31:25.701 --> 00:31:28.746
This is now for
a real programming language,

00:31:28.747 --> 00:31:30.860
so we are working with Java.

00:31:30.861 --> 00:31:35.453
I never try to submit
a paper where I am saying,

00:31:35.454 --> 00:31:38.950
this works beautifully on
these 20 lines of this,

00:31:38.951 --> 00:31:40.655
or these 10 lines of Python.

00:31:40.656 --> 00:31:43.130
For some reason, it has to be

00:31:43.131 --> 00:31:45.993
this big Java program
or this big C program,

00:31:45.994 --> 00:31:48.170
so I am always fearing

00:31:48.171 --> 00:31:50.405
that if I submit something
that works on Python,

00:31:50.406 --> 00:31:52.460
people will not
take it seriously.

00:31:52.461 --> 00:31:54.680
So, we built this whole thing
for Java and it has

00:31:54.681 --> 00:31:57.655
a full grammar inference
of Java programs.

00:31:57.656 --> 00:32:00.305
This is actually
the first technique

00:32:00.306 --> 00:32:04.700
ever that is able to mine
input grammars from programs.

00:32:04.701 --> 00:32:06.740
What we did have before was

00:32:06.741 --> 00:32:08.900
mining such grammar
from samples,

00:32:08.901 --> 00:32:11.802
which is very nice.

00:32:11.803 --> 00:32:17.510
Google has implemented
quite a number

00:32:17.511 --> 00:32:19.770
of machine learning techniques
that tell you that,

00:32:19.771 --> 00:32:22.190
if in a period after command
you have this token and

00:32:22.191 --> 00:32:23.555
this other token then typically

00:32:23.556 --> 00:32:25.460
this next token is
going to follow,

00:32:25.461 --> 00:32:28.945
and you can create
valid documents with that.

00:32:28.946 --> 00:32:30.620
The problem though, is that this

00:32:30.621 --> 00:32:32.422
is not going to tell you well,

00:32:32.423 --> 00:32:33.680
it is only going to give you

00:32:33.681 --> 00:32:35.803
those documents
that look realistic,

00:32:35.804 --> 00:32:38.660
but it is not going to
give you documents that

00:32:38.661 --> 00:32:40.160
explore all the other

00:32:40.161 --> 00:32:41.690
possibilities that
are in the program.

00:32:41.691 --> 00:32:46.390
&gt;&gt; Which paper if
Google does that?

00:32:46.391 --> 00:32:46.850
&gt;&gt; Google does that, yes.

00:32:46.851 --> 00:32:47.724
&gt;&gt; Which paper is that?

00:32:47.725 --> 00:32:51.669
&gt;&gt; I don't know whether they
have a paper but I spoke to

00:32:51.670 --> 00:32:56.274
Domagoj Babic at Google.

00:32:56.275 --> 00:33:00.230
I was not under NDA
and he told me that.

00:33:00.231 --> 00:33:02.300
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]

00:33:02.301 --> 00:33:03.980
&gt;&gt;And there is work
with Patrice Godefroid.

00:33:03.981 --> 00:33:07.348
Yes. Machine learning for
[inaudible] , yes sure.

00:33:07.349 --> 00:33:09.065
So you have the same paper.

00:33:09.066 --> 00:33:12.286
I think they never bothered
to write a paper about it.

00:33:12.287 --> 00:33:15.110
And also there is people at

00:33:15.111 --> 00:33:18.240
Stanford also that
appeared this year at PLDI,

00:33:18.241 --> 00:33:24.335
where people have a great
technique for inferring grammars

00:33:24.336 --> 00:33:27.560
using some active learning
of the program but our idea

00:33:27.561 --> 00:33:29.120
is to have the program as

00:33:29.121 --> 00:33:31.100
a reference so extract
the grammar for it,

00:33:31.101 --> 00:33:34.680
not samples, nothing,
as little as possible.

00:33:34.681 --> 00:33:39.405
Our Java implementation
uses dynamic tainting,

00:33:39.406 --> 00:33:42.605
so we don't look
at memory dumps,

00:33:42.606 --> 00:33:45.320
we simply follow our characters.

00:33:45.321 --> 00:33:47.655
We also do some active learning

00:33:47.656 --> 00:33:50.270
to check for repetitions
and optional parts, that is,

00:33:50.271 --> 00:33:53.269
for each and
every grammar element,

00:33:53.270 --> 00:33:55.265
we actually go and

00:33:55.266 --> 00:33:58.530
find out what happens
if we leave that out.

00:33:58.531 --> 00:34:02.880
And if this still
the result in a valid input,

00:34:02.881 --> 00:34:04.710
if the program under test still

00:34:04.711 --> 00:34:07.035
accepts this, we
know it is optional.

00:34:07.036 --> 00:34:08.550
For every element, we try

00:34:08.551 --> 00:34:11.670
repetitions and if it
accepts multiple repetitions,

00:34:11.671 --> 00:34:13.027
we know we can repeat it.

00:34:13.028 --> 00:34:16.820
That's fine too,
and very nice too.

00:34:16.821 --> 00:34:19.890
We do have a number of
predefined lexical items such as,

00:34:19.891 --> 00:34:23.622
numbers and others
which we identify,

00:34:23.623 --> 00:34:25.515
so these are
regular expressions,

00:34:25.516 --> 00:34:27.570
and if we find out
that some input

00:34:27.571 --> 00:34:29.855
matches a regular expression,

00:34:29.856 --> 00:34:31.410
say such as a number,

00:34:31.411 --> 00:34:33.702
we try out more numbers and

00:34:33.703 --> 00:34:36.205
if we find that more numbers
are also matched,

00:34:36.206 --> 00:34:38.228
we know that this is a number,

00:34:38.229 --> 00:34:43.676
and we also have a hierarchy
of these expressions.

00:34:43.677 --> 00:34:45.240
We have natural numbers and next

00:34:45.241 --> 00:34:47.490
coming up is negative numbers.

00:34:47.491 --> 00:34:49.650
This is how we find out
that the port number,

00:34:49.651 --> 00:34:51.270
for instance, only can
be a natural number

00:34:51.271 --> 00:34:53.130
but cannot be a negative number.

00:34:53.131 --> 00:34:54.450
I have no idea what
happens if you pass

00:34:54.451 --> 00:34:57.205
a negative port number or
whatever is going to happen.

00:34:57.206 --> 00:35:01.115
I remember that if you kill
a negative process on Unix,

00:35:01.116 --> 00:35:04.500
if you kill a process and
give it negative process ID,

00:35:04.501 --> 00:35:05.970
the entire group is going to

00:35:05.971 --> 00:35:07.830
collapse but no idea

00:35:07.831 --> 00:35:09.586
what happens if you
open a negative port.

00:35:09.587 --> 00:35:10.815
Anyway, we now know,

00:35:10.816 --> 00:35:14.059
from our work, that ports
cannot be negative in URL.

00:35:14.060 --> 00:35:16.770
I am going to

00:35:16.771 --> 00:35:18.060
give you a couple of examples

00:35:18.061 --> 00:35:19.605
of what this
actually looks like.

00:35:19.606 --> 00:35:21.720
So, this is again a URL example,

00:35:21.721 --> 00:35:24.870
but now with our autogram
prototype applied on

00:35:24.871 --> 00:35:27.150
the Java net URL parser and

00:35:27.151 --> 00:35:32.755
this is the grammar
that pops out of it.

00:35:32.756 --> 00:35:35.560
This is the raw output
of our tool.

00:35:35.561 --> 00:35:37.110
We just as in

00:35:37.111 --> 00:35:38.940
the Python example that
I have shown you before,

00:35:38.941 --> 00:35:40.830
we make use of
actual variable names and

00:35:40.831 --> 00:35:44.315
function names in order to
make this other readable.

00:35:44.316 --> 00:35:45.765
And here you can
see what is URL?

00:35:45.766 --> 00:35:51.570
A URL starts with a spec
and there is a string in

00:35:51.571 --> 00:35:53.430
the beginning and this string is

00:35:53.431 --> 00:35:55.230
a protocol followed by

00:35:55.231 --> 00:35:58.180
an authority as well
as by an optional path.

00:35:58.181 --> 00:36:01.345
Your authority consists
of an optional password,

00:36:01.346 --> 00:36:03.765
at a host, the host
is mandatory,

00:36:03.766 --> 00:36:06.585
followed by an optional port.

00:36:06.586 --> 00:36:09.885
The protocol in our example
here is always HTTP.

00:36:09.886 --> 00:36:13.675
The user info is a given
but the host is a hostname.

00:36:13.676 --> 00:36:15.690
The port is
a sequence of digits.

00:36:15.691 --> 00:36:17.180
The path is actually a path.

00:36:17.181 --> 00:36:19.890
A query and references are

00:36:19.891 --> 00:36:22.080
all alphanumeric characters with

00:36:22.081 --> 00:36:24.565
special characters in there.

00:36:24.566 --> 00:36:26.260
And this is what we learn from

00:36:26.261 --> 00:36:29.025
this one single example only.

00:36:29.026 --> 00:36:32.620
So, simply by looking at
this one single example,

00:36:32.621 --> 00:36:35.620
which now has a couple of
features and generalizing

00:36:35.621 --> 00:36:38.665
over it and finding out
which parts are optional,

00:36:38.666 --> 00:36:41.005
finding out which parts
can be repeated,

00:36:41.006 --> 00:36:42.760
we get all the
generalizations that

00:36:42.761 --> 00:36:45.110
we get in this anti grammar.

00:36:45.111 --> 00:36:48.110
&gt;&gt; The protocols that are not
[inaudible] they are false,

00:36:48.111 --> 00:36:52.628
have you do some kind
of checking options?

00:36:52.629 --> 00:36:55.240
&gt;&gt; The protocol is something we

00:36:55.241 --> 00:36:58.120
cannot generalize
because our tool

00:36:58.121 --> 00:37:00.730
has no knowledge
about HTTP protocols.

00:37:00.731 --> 00:37:02.980
If it had knowledge
about protocols,

00:37:02.981 --> 00:37:07.435
then I think it would be
cheating at some point.

00:37:07.436 --> 00:37:09.936
We can generalize over
individual protocols,

00:37:09.937 --> 00:37:11.110
what we can find out is

00:37:11.111 --> 00:37:13.210
that we cannot
generalize protocol

00:37:13.211 --> 00:37:14.680
to an arbitrary strain

00:37:14.681 --> 00:37:16.938
because arbitrary strings
would not be accepted.

00:37:16.939 --> 00:37:20.740
But we cannot find out
that other protocols,

00:37:20.741 --> 00:37:23.975
say like HTTPS or file or
something would be accepted.

00:37:23.976 --> 00:37:27.180
We would need
extra inputs for that.

00:37:27.181 --> 00:37:29.345
The reason for that is simple.

00:37:29.346 --> 00:37:32.605
The way the Java URL net
empowers the work is,

00:37:32.606 --> 00:37:34.645
for a given protocol,

00:37:34.646 --> 00:37:36.115
if you pass that in,

00:37:36.116 --> 00:37:39.490
it is going to look
whether there is any class

00:37:39.491 --> 00:37:44.470
in the class loader that
matches the appropriate name,

00:37:44.471 --> 00:37:45.730
and if there is a class,

00:37:45.731 --> 00:37:48.320
it is going to load it and
then it is going to execute it.

00:37:48.321 --> 00:37:54.400
Well, we cannot come up with

00:37:54.401 --> 00:37:58.390
a neat and universal mechanism

00:37:58.391 --> 00:38:00.580
that at some point would
search the file system for

00:38:00.581 --> 00:38:03.320
Java files that make
specific file names.

00:38:03.321 --> 00:38:04.630
So that is not something we can,

00:38:04.631 --> 00:38:05.800
we do not know this.

00:38:05.801 --> 00:38:07.263
But the other parts,

00:38:07.264 --> 00:38:08.440
yes the path, the query,

00:38:08.441 --> 00:38:11.500
reference and all of this
can all be systematically

00:38:11.501 --> 00:38:13.120
expanded such that we know

00:38:13.121 --> 00:38:15.892
which parts are actually
part of the grammar.

00:38:15.893 --> 00:38:18.300
You had a question?

00:38:18.301 --> 00:38:19.020
Not now? Yes.

00:38:19.021 --> 00:38:21.600
&gt;&gt;When you are doing
that team tracking?

00:38:21.601 --> 00:38:22.950
You're now looking at
the path of a thing,

00:38:22.951 --> 00:38:25.950
like the control flow
of a thing.

00:38:25.951 --> 00:38:27.835
&gt;&gt;We're not looking
at the control flow,

00:38:27.836 --> 00:38:29.500
we're only looking
at the data flow.

00:38:29.501 --> 00:38:31.790
Thing is, if we look
at the control flow,

00:38:31.791 --> 00:38:35.180
if we look at control - It's
implicit information flow,

00:38:35.181 --> 00:38:37.620
that would be the correct term.

00:38:37.621 --> 00:38:40.627
Then, we would very quickly get,

00:38:40.628 --> 00:38:43.670
and find out that everything
depends on everything,

00:38:43.671 --> 00:38:46.180
and so we only look
at the data flow.

00:38:46.181 --> 00:38:48.140
This has a limitation.

00:38:48.141 --> 00:38:50.480
For instance,
this technique works

00:38:50.481 --> 00:38:55.685
because the URL parser
is a handwritten parser.

00:38:55.686 --> 00:38:57.260
If we apply this technique

00:38:57.261 --> 00:38:58.765
say on the table driven parser,

00:38:58.766 --> 00:39:00.605
such as a generator parser,

00:39:00.606 --> 00:39:02.660
we would not be able to find

00:39:02.661 --> 00:39:05.180
out that there is
information flow along

00:39:05.181 --> 00:39:07.400
all this part or on
all these table elements

00:39:07.401 --> 00:39:08.720
because all of these are

00:39:08.721 --> 00:39:10.280
governed by implicit
information flow.

00:39:10.281 --> 00:39:11.690
Generally, if you
have something like

00:39:11.691 --> 00:39:13.615
switch and a character,

00:39:13.616 --> 00:39:15.410
and then in case of
that character do that,

00:39:15.411 --> 00:39:17.007
in case of the other
character do that,

00:39:17.008 --> 00:39:18.870
you have a controlled dependency

00:39:18.871 --> 00:39:23.960
and this is something where

00:39:23.961 --> 00:39:27.440
we still have to
tune which kinds

00:39:27.441 --> 00:39:29.360
of control dependencies we

00:39:29.361 --> 00:39:30.080
want to look at and

00:39:30.081 --> 00:39:31.910
which others we don't
want to look at.

00:39:31.911 --> 00:39:34.280
But there is a couple

00:39:34.281 --> 00:39:36.470
of expansions, which
goes into that direction.

00:39:36.471 --> 00:39:38.675
On the other hand, If you do
have a table-driven parser,

00:39:38.676 --> 00:39:39.860
chances are that you do have

00:39:39.861 --> 00:39:41.990
a computer readable grammar in

00:39:41.991 --> 00:39:43.280
the first place
because that's where

00:39:43.281 --> 00:39:46.605
these table-driven parsers
are typically generated from.

00:39:46.606 --> 00:39:48.765
I can give you another example.

00:39:48.766 --> 00:39:50.095
This is actually
a Microsoft one.

00:39:50.096 --> 00:39:53.960
This is INI files. This is
a Java parser for INI files,

00:39:53.961 --> 00:39:56.880
and here we can
see, so INI files.

00:39:56.881 --> 00:39:58.920
That's key value pairs with

00:39:58.921 --> 00:40:03.510
an equal sign sections
in square brackets.

00:40:03.511 --> 00:40:05.510
The essence is in here.

00:40:05.511 --> 00:40:08.535
You have lines that
consist of sections

00:40:08.536 --> 00:40:13.080
or key followed by
arbitrary number of blanks,

00:40:13.081 --> 00:40:14.820
followed by a space,

00:40:14.821 --> 00:40:18.265
followed by an optional value.

00:40:18.266 --> 00:40:20.510
The key is not
optional, the value is.

00:40:20.511 --> 00:40:23.025
I didn't know that you can
have something without values,

00:40:23.026 --> 00:40:24.495
and all of this is in here,

00:40:24.496 --> 00:40:28.155
and these lines then are
separated by new lines,

00:40:28.156 --> 00:40:32.940
and they all are combined
into one single grammar.

00:40:32.941 --> 00:40:35.315
You can see that this
is a bit convoluted.

00:40:35.316 --> 00:40:37.680
You can see here
there's a first line,

00:40:37.681 --> 00:40:40.050
that's first mean parse,
so there's a first section,

00:40:40.051 --> 00:40:42.750
and here again we have
this section down here again.

00:40:42.751 --> 00:40:44.985
That's because of the way
this INI file parser

00:40:44.986 --> 00:40:46.290
is written,

00:40:46.291 --> 00:40:48.410
and I should say it's
not very well written,

00:40:48.411 --> 00:40:55.730
it looks like a crude scanner

00:40:55.731 --> 00:40:59.820
but then these
individual elements

00:40:59.821 --> 00:41:01.590
that are in the
implementation are also

00:41:01.591 --> 00:41:04.425
reflected in our grammar
again but still,

00:41:04.426 --> 00:41:06.615
with a bit of manual cleanup,

00:41:06.616 --> 00:41:10.025
this becomes a very
beautiful grammar.

00:41:10.026 --> 00:41:14.365
Here's my-

00:41:14.366 --> 00:41:16.498
&gt;&gt;You do have
production patterns

00:41:14.367 --> 00:41:15.355
in the sense -

00:41:15.356 --> 00:41:17.657
I would have expected you
to generate things like,

00:41:17.658 --> 00:41:19.960
it's either line 1,
line 2, line 3, line 4

00:41:19.961 --> 00:41:21.960
or line 1, line
2, line 2, line 3

00:41:21.961 --> 00:41:25.350
so you have away to pick
a sequence of lines and

00:41:25.351 --> 00:41:27.350
generalize it to a line start.

00:41:27.351 --> 00:41:28.547
&gt;&gt;Yes. This is what we do too.

00:41:28.548 --> 00:41:30.750
This is a repetition
recognition,

00:41:30.751 --> 00:41:33.480
and we can find out that

00:41:33.481 --> 00:41:35.565
the input is valid
if we have no lines,

00:41:35.566 --> 00:41:36.630
if we have one line,

00:41:36.631 --> 00:41:37.905
and if we have multiple lines,

00:41:37.906 --> 00:41:39.570
and this is how we can
then generalize that

00:41:39.571 --> 00:41:41.300
there is a, is it a clean start?

00:41:41.301 --> 00:41:42.490
I think it's a clean start.

00:41:42.491 --> 00:41:45.780
So we can find out
that the elements can

00:41:45.781 --> 00:41:47.855
be repeated arbitrary
number of time.

00:41:47.856 --> 00:41:50.606
Likewise here for
the spaces and others.

00:41:50.607 --> 00:41:55.550
This is my all favorite.
This is for JSON.

00:41:55.551 --> 00:41:57.390
This is, again,

00:41:57.391 --> 00:42:01.560
one single input so
one single sample suffices.

00:42:01.561 --> 00:42:05.580
This is a constructed example
where we actually look for

00:42:05.581 --> 00:42:08.141
various ways of
number formatting

00:42:08.142 --> 00:42:11.075
because these make up
different parts in the parser.

00:42:11.076 --> 00:42:14.555
Again, we feed this
into our autogram,

00:42:14.556 --> 00:42:17.740
and this is the grammar
that comes out of it,

00:42:17.741 --> 00:42:19.820
and I'm very much in
love with that grammar

00:42:19.821 --> 00:42:23.605
because it's almost
text book quality.

00:42:23.606 --> 00:42:25.880
You see, what is a JSON file?

00:42:25.881 --> 00:42:27.200
The JSON file is the value, and

00:42:27.201 --> 00:42:28.670
the value is either a string,

00:42:28.671 --> 00:42:29.750
or false, or true,

00:42:29.751 --> 00:42:32.320
or an object, an area,
a null, or a number.

00:42:32.321 --> 00:42:34.880
A string is something
called a hash,

00:42:34.881 --> 00:42:37.393
which is a bad name
but I'm happy with it,

00:42:37.394 --> 00:42:39.920
something enclosed
in double quotes.

00:42:39.921 --> 00:42:43.910
False and true are
false and true.

00:42:43.911 --> 00:42:46.535
An object is a string,

00:42:46.536 --> 00:42:49.325
a key value pair followed by

00:42:49.326 --> 00:42:54.255
more optional key value pairs
as many as needed,

00:42:54.256 --> 00:42:57.575
separated by commas and

00:42:57.576 --> 00:43:00.635
with closing
parenthesis at the end.

00:43:00.636 --> 00:43:02.990
An array is similar
except that we're using

00:43:02.991 --> 00:43:07.010
a square brackets
here. Null is a null.

00:43:07.011 --> 00:43:08.870
A number is an integer followed

00:43:08.871 --> 00:43:11.595
by possible fraction
and exponent.

00:43:11.596 --> 00:43:13.215
All of this is in there.

00:43:13.216 --> 00:43:16.400
Again, this is what a tool

00:43:16.401 --> 00:43:20.530
produces from
one single input, okay?

00:43:20.531 --> 00:43:24.515
There's so many things
you can do with that.

00:43:24.516 --> 00:43:26.275
You can use this immediately for

00:43:26.276 --> 00:43:28.310
understanding what
the input is made of,

00:43:28.311 --> 00:43:31.605
because we all know what
a JSON is so we understood.

00:43:31.606 --> 00:43:33.190
We can see that
his grammar is valid,

00:43:33.191 --> 00:43:35.370
but now imagine you have
some unknown file format

00:43:35.371 --> 00:43:37.205
and this is what pops out,

00:43:37.206 --> 00:43:40.150
or you can immediately

00:43:40.151 --> 00:43:41.505
use this for fuzzing
because you can

00:43:41.506 --> 00:43:47.140
immediately create tons and
tons of valid JSON inputs.

00:43:47.141 --> 00:43:49.420
You can use this to compare

00:43:49.421 --> 00:43:52.120
multiple versions of
the program against each other.

00:43:52.121 --> 00:43:53.975
You take one JSON parser,

00:43:53.976 --> 00:43:55.465
take another JSON parser,

00:43:55.466 --> 00:43:56.980
you find out what
the difference between

00:43:56.981 --> 00:43:58.750
the two grammars is so it gives

00:43:58.751 --> 00:44:02.095
you plenty of insights
about how the input is,

00:44:02.096 --> 00:44:03.250
about what the input looks

00:44:03.251 --> 00:44:04.885
like, and what you
can do with it.

00:44:04.886 --> 00:44:07.600
You have a question.

00:44:07.601 --> 00:44:10.145
&gt;&gt;Yes. Terminals or
non-terminals, is that pulled from

00:44:10.146 --> 00:44:11.908
like identifiers within the-.

00:44:11.909 --> 00:44:13.049
&gt;&gt;All pulled from identifiers.

00:44:13.050 --> 00:44:16.535
&gt;&gt;Mostly, there's some
alphanum with white space

00:44:16.536 --> 00:44:18.230
or special white spaces.

00:44:18.231 --> 00:44:21.605
&gt;&gt;The ones that are
underlined here,

00:44:21.606 --> 00:44:23.462
alphanum with
special white space,

00:44:23.463 --> 00:44:26.040
these are our predefined ones.

00:44:26.041 --> 00:44:28.059
I just noticed that string

00:44:28.060 --> 00:44:30.735
internal is not, this
is actually false.

00:44:30.736 --> 00:44:32.480
This is part of
the grammar in here

00:44:32.481 --> 00:44:34.800
but the ones that are
underlined in here,

00:44:34.801 --> 00:44:36.070
this is true up here.

00:44:36.071 --> 00:44:37.790
Integer here and
positive integer,

00:44:37.791 --> 00:44:39.310
these should also be underlined.

00:44:39.311 --> 00:44:40.950
These are our predefined ones.

00:44:40.951 --> 00:44:43.630
We have a paper where
this is described.

00:44:43.631 --> 00:44:44.740
This also come as part of

00:44:44.741 --> 00:44:48.925
another grammar where all
of these are being defined.

00:44:48.926 --> 00:44:50.530
We simply recognize that

00:44:50.531 --> 00:44:53.155
something matches
this regular expression,

00:44:53.156 --> 00:44:56.185
and then we know that this for

00:44:56.186 --> 00:44:57.820
all the positive
integers that we

00:44:57.821 --> 00:44:59.920
have generated actually holds,

00:44:59.921 --> 00:45:03.655
and after a couple of I
think it's five trials,

00:45:03.656 --> 00:45:05.815
after five trials we know well,

00:45:05.816 --> 00:45:08.090
we've seen it work for 53,

00:45:08.091 --> 00:45:10.985
for 4,700 and for three more,

00:45:10.986 --> 00:45:13.375
then we assume it's
a positive integer.

00:45:13.376 --> 00:45:15.370
We can also do
some Newton iteration

00:45:15.371 --> 00:45:16.420
in order to find ranges

00:45:16.421 --> 00:45:17.710
and other stuff that's all in

00:45:17.711 --> 00:45:19.510
the works. You have a question.

00:45:19.511 --> 00:45:24.400
&gt;&gt;Many things or types of
learned object definition.

00:45:24.401 --> 00:45:25.680
&gt;&gt;Yes.

00:45:25.681 --> 00:45:30.480
&gt;&gt;What is the is guideline if
you're trying to understand

00:45:30.481 --> 00:45:33.420
and trying all possible
expressions in the grammar?

00:45:33.421 --> 00:45:39.230
&gt;&gt;What we do for individual
element in one rule, okay?

00:45:39.231 --> 00:45:41.725
For individual elements
in one rule,

00:45:41.726 --> 00:45:43.580
we try to omit them and we

00:45:43.581 --> 00:45:46.325
check whether the input
is still valid.

00:45:46.326 --> 00:45:47.650
Let me just check. I think we

00:45:47.651 --> 00:45:49.150
also do this for larger groups.

00:45:49.151 --> 00:45:50.990
We first do this for
individual humans,

00:45:50.991 --> 00:45:53.565
then for pairs, then
for triples and so on.

00:45:53.566 --> 00:45:56.930
It takes some time so
this JSON inference

00:45:56.931 --> 00:46:00.535
is a number of
a couple of minutes,

00:46:00.536 --> 00:46:02.590
but well, you do it

00:46:02.591 --> 00:46:05.310
once and then you have
it so there you go.

00:46:05.311 --> 00:46:06.110
Yes.

00:46:06.111 --> 00:46:08.490
&gt;&gt;What are you doing when
you obfuscate variable names?

00:46:08.491 --> 00:46:10.490
Let's say someone uses
capture hoops or something

00:46:10.491 --> 00:46:12.490
and then you have
arrays with, you know.

00:46:12.491 --> 00:46:15.085
&gt;&gt; Then all of this
becomes X1, X2,

00:46:15.086 --> 00:46:21.980
X3 and so on so we cannot
find these out either.

00:46:21.981 --> 00:46:24.070
But, even so, the
simple fact that

00:46:24.071 --> 00:46:27.430
it's not only the names
that make these readable,

00:46:27.431 --> 00:46:29.140
it's also the structure
that follows from it

00:46:29.141 --> 00:46:31.945
because you can
really see how this,

00:46:31.946 --> 00:46:33.820
so if you look at
the JSON implementation,

00:46:33.821 --> 00:46:35.770
this structure of
the grammar very nicely

00:46:35.771 --> 00:46:39.520
reflects the structure of

00:46:39.521 --> 00:46:41.575
the program that processes it

00:46:41.576 --> 00:46:45.690
and you can obfuscate
lots of names, no problem.

00:46:45.691 --> 00:46:49.945
If you start obfuscating
the structure of your program,

00:46:49.946 --> 00:46:52.531
that process has inputs.

00:46:52.532 --> 00:46:55.810
Plenty of programmers
who will work

00:46:55.811 --> 00:46:59.550
with your code later on will
be extremely angry at you.

00:46:59.551 --> 00:47:03.220
If you want to really
obfuscate things,

00:47:03.221 --> 00:47:04.360
you also need to come up with

00:47:04.361 --> 00:47:07.225
some mechanism, "Oh,
that's really bad."

00:47:07.226 --> 00:47:08.620
You need to come
up with a mechanism

00:47:08.621 --> 00:47:09.640
that not only obscures

00:47:09.641 --> 00:47:11.260
the control flow but
actually that also

00:47:11.261 --> 00:47:13.750
obscures all the data flow
in your program.

00:47:13.751 --> 00:47:15.850
You would have to split

00:47:15.851 --> 00:47:19.120
these things into multiple
variables, but, even so,

00:47:19.121 --> 00:47:20.860
if you say I'm going to store

00:47:20.861 --> 00:47:22.090
the first three characters in

00:47:22.091 --> 00:47:23.230
one variable and the next

00:47:23.231 --> 00:47:24.870
two characters another variable,

00:47:24.871 --> 00:47:26.800
all that is going to show
up is that there's a rule in

00:47:26.801 --> 00:47:29.440
here that say that false
is composed of two parts,

00:47:29.441 --> 00:47:31.610
and the first is fal
and the second is

00:47:31.611 --> 00:47:35.560
se so yeah, it's a web.

00:47:35.561 --> 00:47:37.420
Right now, there should be ways

00:47:37.421 --> 00:47:39.730
of obscuring this
but right now we're

00:47:39.731 --> 00:47:41.740
rather interested in
bringing more light

00:47:41.741 --> 00:47:44.419
into what's happening here.

00:47:44.420 --> 00:47:48.168
These are just three examples
of a autogram tool,

00:47:48.169 --> 00:47:51.280
and yes, we do have a couple of

00:47:51.281 --> 00:47:54.996
exciting work that's
dealing with that.

00:47:54.997 --> 00:47:58.030
Right now, we are
re-implementing

00:47:58.031 --> 00:48:01.750
this whole thing for
C programs and the reason

00:48:01.751 --> 00:48:07.345
for that is that we found
that our technique for

00:48:07.346 --> 00:48:10.960
tagging the Java
elements and actually

00:48:10.961 --> 00:48:13.390
changing their representation is

00:48:13.391 --> 00:48:15.415
