00:50:17.721 --> 00:50:19.160
out to come up with

00:50:19.161 --> 00:50:21.455
all sorts of various
production rules,

00:50:21.456 --> 00:50:23.965
you get a much better coverage
of the program.

00:50:23.966 --> 00:50:25.460
And that's because
the grammars we're

00:50:25.461 --> 00:50:27.170
having here and the functions,

00:50:27.171 --> 00:50:29.585
that because the structure
of the program,

00:50:29.586 --> 00:50:31.670
is reflected in the structure
of the grammars.

00:50:31.671 --> 00:50:34.160
And so, if you cover
the structure of the grammar,

00:50:34.161 --> 00:50:35.780
you're also going to
cover the structure of

00:50:35.781 --> 00:50:37.940
the program. This
is very helpful.

00:50:37.941 --> 00:50:42.333
Integration with
symbolic testing,

00:50:42.334 --> 00:50:44.145
grammars on one side,

00:50:44.146 --> 00:50:46.580
program semantics
on the other side.

00:50:46.581 --> 00:50:48.810
How do you bring
these two together?

00:50:48.811 --> 00:50:50.580
So what we can do,

00:50:50.581 --> 00:50:52.590
and what one of
my students is working on,

00:50:52.591 --> 00:50:57.855
is to create local solutions

00:50:57.856 --> 00:50:59.685
with symbolic constraint solving

00:50:59.686 --> 00:51:03.690
at the function level that
is determining how can

00:51:03.691 --> 00:51:09.180
you reach a particular
behavior in the program.

00:51:09.181 --> 00:51:11.803
So, solving those constraints,

00:51:11.804 --> 00:51:13.560
and then finding out.

00:51:13.561 --> 00:51:15.660
Well, this variable
which I need to have

00:51:15.661 --> 00:51:17.285
this particular value down here

00:51:17.286 --> 00:51:19.170
in that function in order
to achieve that goal,

00:51:19.171 --> 00:51:22.620
is something that stems from
this element in the grammar,

00:51:22.621 --> 00:51:24.315
because this something
we have established

00:51:24.316 --> 00:51:26.125
earlier with and imitating.

00:51:26.126 --> 00:51:28.080
So we can find out oh,
if you pass a minus

00:51:28.081 --> 00:51:30.425
one down here, it's
going to crash.

00:51:30.426 --> 00:51:32.870
We can lift that value back
to the grammar and see.

00:51:32.871 --> 00:51:35.145
Well, let's put it
in minus one in here.

00:51:35.146 --> 00:51:37.435
Maybe the minus one will
be rejected by the parser,

00:51:37.436 --> 00:51:39.865
but at least that's worth a try.

00:51:39.866 --> 00:51:41.760
And in the end, what
we want to build

00:51:41.761 --> 00:51:44.435
is the world's best
fuzzing platform.

00:51:44.436 --> 00:51:47.130
Yeah I'm competing
with the pros here.

00:51:47.131 --> 00:51:49.830
I know that, but yes,

00:51:49.831 --> 00:51:51.360
we want to build.

00:51:51.361 --> 00:51:54.285
We want to bring all
of this together,

00:51:54.286 --> 00:51:59.640
creating a platform where
you throw in a program,

00:51:59.641 --> 00:52:00.800
where we can infer that in,

00:52:00.801 --> 00:52:04.095
where we can infer
the structure of the input,

00:52:04.096 --> 00:52:06.690
where we can apply
smart test generation techniques

00:52:06.691 --> 00:52:08.460
based on the structure of

00:52:08.461 --> 00:52:10.155
the input and then cover

00:52:10.156 --> 00:52:14.040
as many features as possible
in a minimum amount of time.

00:52:14.041 --> 00:52:17.265
And fortunately, I'm
not alone with that.

00:52:17.266 --> 00:52:20.785
I do have a couple of
great post-docs and PhDs.

00:52:20.786 --> 00:52:23.415
We do have a whole
group in it from

00:52:23.416 --> 00:52:27.830
Oregon State University
student of Alex Gross

00:52:27.831 --> 00:52:30.515
who's working with me

00:52:30.516 --> 00:52:32.760
on the creative part of

00:52:32.761 --> 00:52:35.460
the Nicholas, is
working with Nicholas.

00:52:35.461 --> 00:52:38.160
Avakov is working on
creating the fuzzer.

00:52:38.161 --> 00:52:41.780
Matyas has created the first
implementation of Autogram.

00:52:41.781 --> 00:52:42.960
Alexander is working on

00:52:42.961 --> 00:52:44.565
integration with
symbolic testing,

00:52:44.566 --> 00:52:49.440
and Burin is doing all the work
on C programs these days.

00:52:49.441 --> 00:52:55.570
This is the army of folks
who is helping with it.

00:52:55.571 --> 00:52:57.750
I'd like to point out that
I wrote the Python programs.

00:52:57.751 --> 00:53:01.530
So, you know, for

00:53:01.531 --> 00:53:03.420
somebody who
increasingly relies on

00:53:03.421 --> 00:53:05.640
others to write programs
and build stuff,

00:53:05.641 --> 00:53:07.410
I would like to point

00:53:07.411 --> 00:53:09.510
out that I also can
write stuff myself.

00:53:09.511 --> 00:53:13.530
So, just to point that out,
just to point that out.

00:53:13.531 --> 00:53:15.620
German professors
can write code too.

00:53:15.621 --> 00:53:16.740
You are happy to analyze my

00:53:16.741 --> 00:53:18.180
Python code for
whatever features.

00:53:18.181 --> 00:53:23.725
Occasionally, I write stuff OK?

00:53:23.726 --> 00:53:27.205
And in the long run, you
know that this is fun.

00:53:27.206 --> 00:53:29.915
I looked at file formats
in Wikipedia.

00:53:29.916 --> 00:53:31.645
There's a page that's
called file format.

00:53:31.646 --> 00:53:33.020
There's a list of file format.

00:53:33.021 --> 00:53:34.645
These are all the
file formats that are

00:53:34.646 --> 00:53:36.360
documented somewhere
in Wikipedia.

00:53:36.361 --> 00:53:38.495
There is about, I think

00:53:38.496 --> 00:53:41.235
it's 1,000 different
file formats,

00:53:41.236 --> 00:53:44.070
all possible three and
four letter combinations.

00:53:44.071 --> 00:53:47.225
File extensions are written
in their JPEG format.

00:53:47.226 --> 00:53:49.430
You don't know
everything under the sun.

00:53:49.431 --> 00:53:51.710
Dotsie are download,
no idea what this is,

00:53:51.711 --> 00:53:53.400
oh it's Google Chrome of course.

00:53:53.401 --> 00:53:55.410
And we wanted to be able to

00:53:55.411 --> 00:53:59.340
infer those formats
one after the other,

00:53:59.341 --> 00:54:03.865
and then be able
to fuzz them all.

00:54:03.866 --> 00:54:06.895
As I said this is
very simple question:

00:54:06.896 --> 00:54:09.705
what is the input language
of a program?

00:54:09.706 --> 00:54:11.115
This is a very simple question,

00:54:11.116 --> 00:54:13.935
and it's awfully
under-researched.

00:54:13.936 --> 00:54:17.330
It's a question that, well,

00:54:17.331 --> 00:54:19.740
it's very fundamental
to all sorts of testing

00:54:19.741 --> 00:54:22.170
and of course to
program understanding,

00:54:22.171 --> 00:54:23.610
at least of data understanding,

00:54:23.611 --> 00:54:27.460
but inferring this
automatically is something that

00:54:27.461 --> 00:54:30.900
will at best a handful

00:54:30.901 --> 00:54:34.520
of people who've undertaken
into the last decade.

00:54:34.521 --> 00:54:36.630
And how can I leverage

00:54:36.631 --> 00:54:38.910
this infrastructure
in order to cover,

00:54:38.911 --> 00:54:41.100
understand, prevent
particular inputs

00:54:41.101 --> 00:54:43.570
if have a parser force
but for particular inputs?

00:54:43.571 --> 00:54:44.845
Then you can say, "Okay,

00:54:44.846 --> 00:54:46.710
here's this legacy program

00:54:46.711 --> 00:54:48.145
which has a couple
of vulnerabilities.

00:54:48.146 --> 00:54:49.980
I'm going to
restrict its input to

00:54:49.981 --> 00:54:52.410
only those inputs that I know

00:54:52.411 --> 00:54:55.390
that are legal or
the other way around.

00:54:55.391 --> 00:54:57.680
I know that specific inputs
are malicious.

00:54:57.681 --> 00:54:59.185
I'm going to infer
grammar for this.

00:54:59.186 --> 00:55:02.175
I'm going to explicitly
prevent them." There you go.

00:55:02.176 --> 00:55:04.050
You don't even have to patch

00:55:04.051 --> 00:55:05.580
the program for your whatever,

00:55:05.581 --> 00:55:08.850
for your two-year-old
IOT device which

00:55:08.851 --> 00:55:11.460
you bought at a cheap price

00:55:11.461 --> 00:55:12.750
and which no longer
is maintained.

00:55:12.751 --> 00:55:15.450
Well, at least you can
set up a filter for that.

00:55:15.451 --> 00:55:16.985
All of this translate also into

00:55:16.986 --> 00:55:19.380
better understanding of code
because if you know that

00:55:19.381 --> 00:55:23.680
this part of code processes
this particular input,

00:55:23.681 --> 00:55:25.080
well, you have

00:55:25.081 --> 00:55:27.100
a deeper understanding
of what is going on.

00:55:27.101 --> 00:55:28.830
And of course, this
also gives you an idea

00:55:28.831 --> 00:55:31.920
about how to cover
specific behaviors,

00:55:31.921 --> 00:55:34.205
how to understand behaviors.

00:55:34.206 --> 00:55:37.110
There's hundreds of
open issues that it's

00:55:37.111 --> 00:55:41.290
very easy to start
doing research.

00:55:41.291 --> 00:55:43.985
If I can write a Python program
that infers a grammar in

00:55:43.986 --> 00:55:46.675
four hours, you can do so.

00:55:46.676 --> 00:55:48.310
I'm sure you folks can do so in

00:55:48.311 --> 00:55:51.055
one hour including
learning Python.

00:55:51.056 --> 00:55:55.135
So, please enjoy it all, okay.

00:55:55.136 --> 00:55:56.890
And this gets me to
the end of my talk.

00:55:56.891 --> 00:55:58.620
So, what have I shown?

00:55:58.621 --> 00:56:00.610
I've shown you how
to use grammars

00:56:00.611 --> 00:56:03.890
for fuzzing of
existing programs.

00:56:03.891 --> 00:56:05.440
The obstacle to that

00:56:05.441 --> 00:56:07.775
is getting a grammar
in the first place.

00:56:07.776 --> 00:56:10.510
And I have shown you, well,

00:56:10.511 --> 00:56:12.280
both the Python
prototype as well as

00:56:12.281 --> 00:56:15.700
our Autogram prototype that

00:56:15.701 --> 00:56:18.678
is able to infer
such grammars automatically,

00:56:18.679 --> 00:56:21.520
and the current work
of my students

00:56:21.521 --> 00:56:24.850
happily working on extending

00:56:24.851 --> 00:56:26.640
this idea in various directions.

00:56:26.641 --> 00:56:30.010
And there is plenty of research
opportunities around it,

00:56:30.011 --> 00:56:32.725
and all of this can
actually be found

00:56:32.726 --> 00:56:35.820
on our web page that
is devoted to Autogram.

00:56:35.821 --> 00:56:39.790
You also find our most
recent technical report with

00:56:39.791 --> 00:56:45.780
all the features on
active learning there.

00:56:45.781 --> 00:56:47.680
So, was it fun?

00:56:47.681 --> 00:56:49.655
It was my pleasure to present,

00:56:49.656 --> 00:56:51.100
and I'm happy to take questions.

00:56:51.101 --> 00:56:55.990
Thank you very much
for your attention.

00:56:55.991 --> 00:56:59.365
&gt;&gt; Yes, Chris?

00:56:59.366 --> 00:57:03.280
&gt;&gt; Really cool,

00:57:03.281 --> 00:57:04.880
I really like the topic,

00:57:03.282 --> 00:57:04.946
and the result is
kind of exciting.

00:57:04.947 --> 00:57:09.413
My question is, inputs,

00:57:09.414 --> 00:57:12.400
it seems like you focusing
mostly in input that is

00:57:12.401 --> 00:57:17.354
human readable and often inputs,

00:57:17.355 --> 00:57:20.652
whether it's file formats or
across the wire or binary,

00:57:20.653 --> 00:57:21.880
typically, at least
in my experience,

00:57:21.881 --> 00:57:23.860
writing the codecs
for these things,

00:57:23.861 --> 00:57:25.280
they are not context-free.

00:57:25.281 --> 00:57:25.630
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:57:25.631 --> 00:57:27.230
&gt;&gt; You'll have some

00:57:27.231 --> 00:57:29.130
length encoded or layers
something like that.

00:57:29.131 --> 00:57:29.480
&gt;&gt; Yes. Yes. Yes. Yes

00:57:29.481 --> 00:57:31.330
&gt;&gt;Do you have a sense

00:57:31.331 --> 00:57:33.417
of how well these types
of approaches would

00:57:33.418 --> 00:57:35.670
work in trying to handle that

00:57:35.671 --> 00:57:37.190
where the output is not

00:57:37.191 --> 00:57:40.070
context-free grammar
but something else?

00:57:40.071 --> 00:57:44.650
&gt;&gt; Yes. There's
two important issues in there.

00:57:44.651 --> 00:57:48.460
The first one is
context, as you put it.

00:57:48.461 --> 00:57:49.750
What we infer here is context

00:57:49.751 --> 00:57:51.160
through grammars and there's

00:57:51.161 --> 00:57:55.330
plenty of data formats.

00:57:55.331 --> 00:57:58.940
Actually, in
most reasonable formats,

00:57:58.941 --> 00:58:02.800
there is some amount of
context sensitivity in there.

00:58:02.801 --> 00:58:04.645
You would normally not want

00:58:04.646 --> 00:58:07.915
a context-sensitive grammar
to describe these.

00:58:07.916 --> 00:58:09.520
You can say, for instance,

00:58:09.521 --> 00:58:12.340
suppose you have a checksum
for instance, okay?

00:58:12.341 --> 00:58:14.575
You do not want to describe

00:58:14.576 --> 00:58:18.078
the checksum using a
context-sensitive grammar, okay?

00:58:18.079 --> 00:58:23.285
You can do that
but that's not fun.

00:58:23.286 --> 00:58:26.515
I mean you look at
regular expressions and say,

00:58:26.516 --> 00:58:30.100
I'm not sure about that
but programmers love

00:58:30.101 --> 00:58:31.420
regular expressions and for

00:58:31.421 --> 00:58:34.153
100 regular expressions
you have one grammar,

00:58:34.154 --> 00:58:35.785
one context-free
grammar that is.

00:58:35.786 --> 00:58:38.365
I'm pretty sure that for
100 context-free grammars,

00:58:38.366 --> 00:58:41.425
you have one context-sensitive
grammar and then we go up

00:58:41.426 --> 00:58:42.840
the way to universal grammar

00:58:42.841 --> 00:58:44.434
and Turing machines and whatnot.

00:58:44.435 --> 00:58:46.210
The further up you go,

00:58:46.211 --> 00:58:48.045
the less popular things are

00:58:48.046 --> 00:58:49.150
and you don't want to write

00:58:49.151 --> 00:58:50.700
this with a
context-sensitive grammar.

00:58:50.701 --> 00:58:52.090
What we're working on is to

00:58:52.091 --> 00:58:55.175
extend such context-free
grammars with constraints,

00:58:55.176 --> 00:58:59.950
that tell you that one
particular item is the result of

00:58:59.951 --> 00:59:04.743
this particular operation being
applied on this other item

00:59:04.744 --> 00:59:06.040
and this operation is

00:59:06.041 --> 00:59:09.820
something that we imagine
can actually be extracted

00:59:09.821 --> 00:59:12.000
from code because
you can find out that

00:59:12.001 --> 00:59:15.250
the length that is
being encoded in here,

00:59:15.251 --> 00:59:16.735
actually comes from code

00:59:16.736 --> 00:59:18.350
and is being checked
at this point,

00:59:18.351 --> 00:59:21.115
so we can extract
these operations

00:59:21.116 --> 00:59:24.790
and feed them and make
constraints out of them.

00:59:24.791 --> 00:59:29.830
This is how you can then
represent constraints with

00:59:29.831 --> 00:59:31.847
respect to checksums
and length and of

00:59:31.848 --> 00:59:35.715
all these other container
formats and binary formats.

00:59:35.716 --> 00:59:38.220
Actually,

00:59:38.221 --> 00:59:40.180
I have a very nice
master thesis on

00:59:40.181 --> 00:59:42.405
that where I'm
awaiting results soon,

00:59:42.406 --> 00:59:47.440
which is specifically geared
towards checksums and input.

00:59:47.441 --> 00:59:51.140
The second thing is
that what we need here,

00:59:51.141 --> 00:59:53.410
what we have here is

00:59:53.411 --> 00:59:58.750
actually multiple layers of
languages stacked upon each

00:59:58.751 --> 01:00:02.895
other like in
media formats you have or

01:00:02.896 --> 01:00:07.205
say like the ISO/OSI
networking model,

01:00:07.206 --> 01:00:09.210
not sure whether this
is still in textbooks.

01:00:09.211 --> 01:00:11.020
Let's say you have
a layer where describe

01:00:11.021 --> 01:00:13.240
things in terms of
bits being processed,

01:00:13.241 --> 01:00:15.655
you have a layer where
you think about these as

01:00:15.656 --> 01:00:18.700
data packets and then
somewhere up there,

01:00:18.701 --> 01:00:20.560
you have the application layer.

01:00:20.561 --> 01:00:22.240
Same thing happens
with media containers.

01:00:22.241 --> 01:00:23.500
You have a
representation where you

01:00:23.501 --> 01:00:24.940
have little bits
with checksums and

01:00:24.941 --> 01:00:26.200
lengths and then you have

01:00:26.201 --> 01:00:28.270
another representation where
you actually talk about

01:00:28.271 --> 01:00:29.430
the context of all these

01:00:29.431 --> 01:00:30.790
individual frames
that are packaged in

01:00:30.791 --> 01:00:35.170
here and our goal is to
bring these together,

01:00:35.171 --> 01:00:37.055
not only and by having
multiple grammar.

01:00:37.056 --> 01:00:40.200
We can have one grammar for
each layer that describes it,

01:00:40.201 --> 01:00:45.280
but the concept of
so-called conjunctive grammars,

01:00:45.281 --> 01:00:49.480
which is a mostly
theoretical result from

01:00:49.481 --> 01:00:51.880
language theory but the idea is

01:00:51.881 --> 01:00:55.150
that you can take
multiple constraints.

01:00:55.151 --> 01:00:59.870
The idea is that not only
you have these junctions.

01:00:59.871 --> 01:01:02.220
I'll tell you what
this can be I or B,

01:01:02.221 --> 01:01:04.150
but you can also
have conjunctions.

01:01:04.151 --> 01:01:06.310
You can tell this can be A or B

01:01:06.311 --> 01:01:09.490
and it also has to fulfill
this other constraint and

01:01:09.491 --> 01:01:11.170
the idea here is
that you can then

01:01:11.171 --> 01:01:13.855
express that file has to

01:01:13.856 --> 01:01:16.480
fulfill the constrains
that happen at

01:01:16.481 --> 01:01:17.860
the bit level but also at

01:01:17.861 --> 01:01:19.885
the application level and
all of these together.

01:01:19.886 --> 01:01:21.665
You can have negations
in there actually,

01:01:21.666 --> 01:01:23.290
then you get Boolean grammar.

01:01:23.291 --> 01:01:25.930
Then you can say, an input
has to fulfill for this,

01:01:25.931 --> 01:01:29.015
and this, and must
not be this, okay?

01:01:29.016 --> 01:01:32.335
Then you can actually
combine things like,

01:01:32.336 --> 01:01:35.669
this is a kind of input that
you'd like to avoid so you

01:01:35.670 --> 01:01:39.530
have an explicit blacklist
for instance.

01:01:39.531 --> 01:01:41.665
This is something that we have,

01:01:41.666 --> 01:01:47.140
that where we think
of in the near future,

01:01:47.141 --> 01:01:49.220
we'll be able to infer these.

01:01:49.221 --> 01:01:51.480
The problem is
production because,

01:01:51.481 --> 01:01:53.470
right now, if you only
have these junctions,

01:01:53.471 --> 01:01:55.200
production is
a very simple thing.

01:01:55.201 --> 01:01:56.883
As soon as you
have conjunctions,

01:01:56.884 --> 01:01:57.740
you actually have to go into

01:01:57.741 --> 01:01:59.045
string constraint solving and

01:01:59.046 --> 01:02:01.540
others where you, in the end,

01:02:01.541 --> 01:02:03.040
have to invoke something like

01:02:03.041 --> 01:02:06.600
a humpy or Z three in order
to fulfill those constraints.

01:02:06.601 --> 01:02:12.395
But, again, this is classical.

01:02:12.396 --> 01:02:14.734
This is a classical
constraint solving.

01:02:14.735 --> 01:02:15.750
We have tools for
that, we know how to do

01:02:15.751 --> 01:02:18.195
that so it's not
out of the reach.

01:02:18.196 --> 01:02:23.785
Two answers, two longer answers
to a two single question.

01:02:23.786 --> 01:02:25.710
Yes?

01:02:25.711 --> 01:02:26.600
&gt;&gt; The examples that you

01:02:26.601 --> 01:02:27.785
show would had
been the proper way

01:02:27.786 --> 01:02:31.077
with this thing, in the
first, in writing any grammar.

01:02:31.078 --> 01:02:31.080
&gt;&gt; Sorry.

01:02:31.081 --> 01:02:32.261
&gt;&gt; Would it have been

01:02:32.262 --> 01:02:33.530
the proper way to deal

01:02:33.531 --> 01:02:35.000
with these things
to write grammar in

01:02:35.001 --> 01:02:40.710
the first place rather than
constructing that as you go?

01:02:40.711 --> 01:02:44.438
&gt;&gt; Well, you know, even for
a simple thing as a URL,

01:02:44.439 --> 01:02:45.800
writing a grammar can be

01:02:45.801 --> 01:02:49.400
difficult because if
you write the grammar,

01:02:49.401 --> 01:02:50.900
you start to reason about

01:02:50.901 --> 01:02:53.105
what kind of characters
can be in the path?

01:02:53.106 --> 01:02:55.349
What is the range of
the port? Can I omit the port?

01:02:55.350 --> 01:02:57.140
What kind of characters

01:02:57.141 --> 01:02:59.445
are allowed in
the password, okay?

01:02:59.446 --> 01:03:04.490
This is the things that
make you think hard but it's

01:03:04.491 --> 01:03:06.975
important to capture
these constraints

01:03:06.976 --> 01:03:08.270
precisely because these are

01:03:08.271 --> 01:03:10.479
precisely the things
you want to test,

01:03:10.480 --> 01:03:14.690
and by having an automatic
learner, or in this case,

01:03:14.691 --> 01:03:17.690
an active learner that
infers those things for

01:03:17.691 --> 01:03:21.500
you by running tens of
thousands of experiments.

01:03:21.501 --> 01:03:23.390
You get way more
information than what

01:03:23.391 --> 01:03:26.055
you could ever do through
manual testing and probing.

01:03:26.056 --> 01:03:28.575
Even for a symbol,

01:03:28.576 --> 01:03:30.580
even for for something
where we think we

01:03:30.581 --> 01:03:32.990
know how something is composed,

01:03:32.991 --> 01:03:38.470
formalizing this is
actually manual work.

01:03:38.471 --> 01:03:42.620
If you think about a format
that you do not know,

01:03:42.621 --> 01:03:47.855
okay, and say some
proprietary data format.

01:03:47.856 --> 01:03:49.823
I remember, I was working,

01:03:49.824 --> 01:03:51.630
this was during my student term,

01:03:51.631 --> 01:03:53.800
I was working with
a with some sort

01:03:53.801 --> 01:03:56.440
of vector drawing program,

01:03:56.441 --> 01:03:58.360
which had a
proprietary file format

01:03:58.361 --> 01:04:01.100
in which everything was stored.

01:04:01.101 --> 01:04:02.443
I had no idea what
this looked like.

01:04:02.444 --> 01:04:04.160
I loaded this file into

01:04:04.161 --> 01:04:06.020
my text editor and

01:04:06.021 --> 01:04:09.236
the first characters
in that file were,

01:04:09.237 --> 01:04:11.925
so you're trying to find
out the innards of a

01:04:11.926 --> 01:04:15.845
such and such file
A, question mark.

01:04:15.846 --> 01:04:17.555
It was an insult.

01:04:17.556 --> 01:04:18.980
I mean it's like you abandon all

01:04:18.981 --> 01:04:21.240
of you who enter here
or something like that.

01:04:21.241 --> 01:04:22.973
Okay, so it is difficult.

01:04:22.974 --> 01:04:24.360
Now, you think about,

01:04:24.361 --> 01:04:26.150
somewhere there's a hard disk in

01:04:26.151 --> 01:04:28.070
which somebody has stored
millions and millions of

01:04:28.071 --> 01:04:30.520
pictures made with
that particular program on

01:04:30.521 --> 01:04:33.395
a hard disk and you have
no idea what it is made of,

01:04:33.396 --> 01:04:38.705
but if we can extract
the grammar for it,

01:04:38.706 --> 01:04:40.760
then we will be able to

01:04:40.761 --> 01:04:42.840
identify what it's
made of and actually,

01:04:42.841 --> 01:04:44.600
if you think of
it, we can create

01:04:44.601 --> 01:04:47.030
a universal XML converter,

01:04:47.031 --> 01:04:49.850
take anything and
convert it to XML using

01:04:49.851 --> 01:04:53.560
this very structure in the
grammar to use XML out of it,

01:04:53.561 --> 01:04:56.665
and all of a sudden you
can reuse everything.

01:04:56.666 --> 01:05:03.350
Yes, there's plenty
of uses for that.

01:05:03.351 --> 01:05:05.020
&gt;&gt; People might want to replace

01:05:05.021 --> 01:05:06.888
their current implementation,

01:05:06.889 --> 01:05:07.277
their grammar- based
implementation that they get

01:05:07.278 --> 01:05:09.840
off of your tools.

01:05:09.841 --> 01:05:11.590
&gt;&gt; Well, if you have
a grammar then it's

01:05:11.591 --> 01:05:14.530
easier to create
a parser again out of it.

01:05:14.531 --> 01:05:16.300
It's obviously easy to-

01:05:16.301 --> 01:05:20.110
also if you think
of it as an Oracle,

01:05:20.111 --> 01:05:22.240
for instance, you can
use the grammar to check

01:05:22.241 --> 01:05:24.825
whether the outputs
are valid for instance.

01:05:24.826 --> 01:05:30.966
It's amazing that we as well,

01:05:30.967 --> 01:05:34.970
computer scientists
love grammars, we do.

01:05:34.971 --> 01:05:39.640
If you do one year of
studying computer science,

01:05:39.641 --> 01:05:41.950
you do learn a bit
about grammars in

01:05:41.951 --> 01:05:44.795
formal languages and
you need them later too.

01:05:44.796 --> 01:05:48.938
But bringing
these things together,

01:05:48.939 --> 01:05:51.275
here's my program and
here's the language.

01:05:51.276 --> 01:05:53.620
Okay, so far, we've only

01:05:53.621 --> 01:05:55.930
seen this from
the prospect of creating

01:05:55.931 --> 01:05:58.285
a specification and
then implementing it

01:05:58.286 --> 01:06:00.820
and creating a parser

01:06:00.821 --> 01:06:03.790
automatically from
a written specification.

01:06:03.791 --> 01:06:05.800
Yes, we know that this
is parser generator

01:06:05.801 --> 01:06:07.548
but the other way around,

01:06:07.549 --> 01:06:09.800
here's a program
what's its input?

01:06:09.801 --> 01:06:11.800
That's a problem
that has never been

01:06:11.801 --> 01:06:14.320
addressed before
and I'm not even

01:06:14.321 --> 01:06:17.200
sure whether the problem
has been posed before

01:06:17.201 --> 01:06:18.700
and I think the problem has not

01:06:18.701 --> 01:06:20.585
been posed before
because so far.

01:06:20.586 --> 01:06:24.190
Even though the answer
is relatively simple,

01:06:24.191 --> 01:06:26.560
it simply was not
obvious how to do that.

01:06:26.561 --> 01:06:27.725
The thing is if you apply

01:06:27.726 --> 01:06:30.795
standard static symbolic
techniques and have a parse.

01:06:30.796 --> 01:06:33.220
If you have a parser
and you want to find

01:06:33.221 --> 01:06:34.360
out what it does using

01:06:34.361 --> 01:06:36.445
standard static
symbolic techniques.

01:06:36.446 --> 01:06:38.020
You ever looked at
a generated parser

01:06:38.021 --> 01:06:39.100
with its tables and everything,

01:06:39.101 --> 01:06:41.350
it's impossible
to understand what

01:06:41.351 --> 01:06:43.665
it does and also
for constraints,

01:06:43.666 --> 01:06:45.470
it's impossible because if you

01:06:45.471 --> 01:06:47.385
have a table with
10,000 entries,

01:06:47.386 --> 01:06:49.540
you're going to end up with
a constraint that has 10,000

01:06:49.541 --> 01:06:52.610
entries until the solution
is lo and behold the grammar.

01:06:52.611 --> 01:06:55.090
So there was
no such thing and only

01:06:55.091 --> 01:06:57.990
by using a dynamic
technique I think,

01:06:57.991 --> 01:07:00.130
by looking at what's
actually happening during

01:07:00.131 --> 01:07:02.050
the execution and combining this

01:07:02.051 --> 01:07:04.180
with active learning and
testing this is how we can know.

01:07:04.181 --> 01:07:06.535
Now, we have a good grip on

01:07:06.536 --> 01:07:09.453
how things and how to
tackle that problem.

01:07:09.454 --> 01:07:11.920
It's still going
to be a decade of

01:07:11.921 --> 01:07:15.580
research until it all
works out and there's

01:07:15.581 --> 01:07:19.950
plenty of opportunities so
I'm happy to encourage you all

01:07:19.951 --> 01:07:24.940
to try these things
out for yourself.

01:07:24.941 --> 01:07:29.100
&gt;&gt; Okay. So let's thank
Andreas one more time.

01:07:29.101 --> 01:07:33.100
&gt;&gt; Pleasure.

